ry of Ireland | Irish Law Links | Law Society | British & Irish Legal Information Institute | Offshore Business Laws | Irish legislation Italy: Constitution | Constitutional Court (Italian) | Alternate Site | Decisions of the Constitutional Court (Italian)| Ministry of Justice (Italian) | Parliament (Italian) | Embassy | Other Legal Materials | Patent & Trademark Office (Italian) | Intellectual Property Resources | Arbitration Law | Telecommunications Regulation | Italian Competition Authority | Italian Law on the Web (Italian) | Italian Law Latvia: Constitution | Alternate copy | Parliament | President | Constitutional Court | Constitutional Court Decisions | Other Legal Materials | Law & Politics | Citizens' Rights | Law Links | Latvian legislation Liechtenstein: Constitution (1921 - 2003) | 2003 Revised Constitution (German) | Government | Parliament | National Administration | The Princely House of Liechtenstein | Liechtenstein Collection of Laws (German) | Jurisdiction & Courts | Offshore Business Laws | Research Guide to the Law of Liechtenstein Lithuania: Constitution | Alternate copy | Other constitutional materials | Constitutional Court | Constitutional Court Decisions| Ministry of Justice | Parliament | Legal Acts (searchable database) | Some Basic Laws | President | Prime Minister | Law & Politics | Government Index | Patent Bureau & Laws | Lithuanian legislation Luxembourg: Constitution | Government (French) | Prime Minister (French) | Chamber of Deputies (French) | Other Legal Materials | Telecommunications Law | IP Law | Offshore Business Laws | Benelux Trademark Office Macedonia: Constitution | Alternate copy | Alternate copy | Government | President | Ministry of Justice (Macedonian) | Ministry of Economy | Macedonian Laws | Law & Politics | Macedonian Code of Judicial Ethics | Election Law (President) | Election Law (Assembly) | Macedonian legislation Malta: Constitution | Constitutional Court Decisions (Maltese) | Government | Parliament | Prime Minister | Other Legal Materials | Maltese Laws | Offshore Business Laws | Lawyers' Code of Ethics & Conduct | Maltese Law Page Moldova: Constitution | Alternate copy | Alternate copy | President | Parliament | Constitutional Court | Electoral Code | Legislation (Moldovan, Russian) | Law & Politics | Moldova Official Site | Moldovan legislation Netherlands: Constitution | Parliament | Government | Supreme Court (Dutch) | Researching Dutch Law | IP Law | Arbitration Law | Penal Code | Ministry of Justice | Other legal resources | Dutch legislation Norway: Constitution | Alternate copy | Alternate copy (Norwegian) | National Government Site | Supreme Court | Supreme Court Decisions (Norwegian) | Additional Supreme Court Decisions (Norwegian) | Appellate Court Decisions (Norwegian) | Parliament | Statutes (Norwegian) | Norwegian Legislation Search (Norwegian) | Norwegian Laws in English | Central Regulations (Norwegian) | Prime Minister | Bankruptcy Act (PDF) Poland: Constitution | Alternate copy | Summary of key provisions | Government | Constitutional Tribunal | Decisions of Constitutional Tribunal | Supreme Court | Decisions of Supreme Court | Senate | Lower Chamber (Sejm) | President | Prime Minister | Other Legal Materials | Overview of Polish Law | Substantive Criminal Law | Law & Politics | Polish legislation Portugal: Constitution (1989) | Alternate copy (1992) | Decisions of Supreme Court (Portuguese) | Constitutional Court (Portuguese) | Assembly of the Republic | Other legal materials (Portuguese) | Prime Minister | Attorney General | IP Office (Portuguese) | IP Law | Legal Materials | Legislation (Portuguese) Romania: Constitution | Alternate copy | Alternate copy | Government | Constitutional Court | Other constitutional materials | Senate (Romanian) | Chamber of Deputies | Legislation | Romanian Laws | President | Other legal materials | Law & Politics | Patent & Trademark Office | Law-Counsel.com | Doing Legal Research in Romania Russia: Constitution | Alternate copy | Alternate copy | Alternate copy | Constitutional Court (Russian) | Supreme Court (Russian) | Federation Council | State Duma (Russian) | Russian Laws | Government (Russian) | President | Law & Politics | Russian Legal Information | Russian Space Policy | Federal Election Law | Russian Law on Religion | Russian legislation | Moscow Patent Bureau Slovakia: Constitution | Alternate copy | Alternate copy | Government | Constitutional Court | National Council | President | Other legal materials | Law & Politics | Industrial Property Office Slovenia: Constitution | Constitutional Court | All courts | Case law search | Case law search (comprehensive, Slovenian) | National Assembly | National Council | President | Banking Laws | Government | Ministries | Other government links | Other Legal materials | Law & Politics Spain: Constitution | Alternate Copy (PDF) | Alternate copy (Spanish) | Constitutional Court (Spanish) | Constitutional Law Links (Spanish) | General Council of the Judiciary (Spanish) | Legislation | Government (Spanish) | President | Congress of Deputies | Senate | Autonomous Governments | Council of Ministers | Other legal information (Spanish) | Patent & Trademark Office | IP Law | Health Law (Spanish) | Telecommunication Law (Spanish) | General Resources Sweden: Constitution | Decisions of the Supreme Court (Swedish) | Parliament | Statutes | Penal Code (PDF) | Government | Government Offices | Other legal materials | Swedish Bar Association | Patent & Registration Office | IP Law | Arbitration Law | Environmental Code (PDF) | Swedish legislation Switzerland: Constitution | Federal Supreme Court (French, German, Italian) | Decisions of the Federal Supreme Court (French, German, Italian) | Federal Assembly | Federal Authority | Private International Law Statute (French, German, Italian) | Offshore Business Laws | Swiss Law Net (French, German) | Swiss legislation | Federal Institute of Intellectual Property Ukraine: Constitution | Government | President | Parliament | Laws (Ukrainian) | Other Legal Materials | Law & Politics | Arbitration Law | Business Law | Ukraine Substantive Law | Ukraine legislation | State Department of Intellectual Property United Kingdom: Magna Carta | Courts and decisions | Government | High Court and Court of Appeal cases (BAILII)| Civil Procedure Rules | Prime Minister | Parliament | UK Legislation | Acts of Parliament | Statutory Instruments | House of Lords | Judgments of the House of Lords | Arbitration Act| IP Law | More IP Law | Law by Subject | Wales Legal Information | Northern Ireland Assembly | Statutory Rules of Northern Ireland | Northern Ireland Legislation | Northern Ireland Legal Information | Scottish Parliament | Statutory Rules of Scotland | Scotland Legislation | Scottish Courts | National Assembly of Wales | Statutory Rules of Wales | Scotland Legal Information | UK Legal system | Courts & Case Law | Legislation Links Serbia ( Republic of Serbia): Legal Information System of Republic of Serbia | Database of Legal Documents | Official Gazette | National Assembly of Republic of Serbia | President of Republic of Serbia | Government of Republic of Serbia | Judicial Portal | Constitutional Court | Supreme Court | Autonomous Province of Vojvodina Official Journal of Autonomous Province of Vojvodina | Assembly of Autonomous Province of Vojvodina | Government of Autonomous Province of Vojvodina | Cities and Municipalities Beograd | Kragujevac | Ni\u0161 | Novi Sad Publishers Intermex | ING-PRO | Paragraf Lex NPO Serbian Bar Association | Vojvodina Bar Association Montenegro: Parliament of Montenegro | Government of Montenegro | Courts of Montenegro | Montenegro Bar Association European Union: Europa (EU's Server) | Council of the European Union | European Commission | European Parliament | European Court of Justice | Economic and Social Committee | Treaties, Legislation, and Case Law (EUR-Lex) | Founding Treaties | Green Papers | White Papers | European Documentation Center (German) | Press Releases | European Investment Bank | European Central Bank | European Union Presidency | Ombudsman | European Union Links | European Data Protection Supervisor | Open Data Other Regional Institutions: Council of Europe | European Court of Human Rights | Organization for Security and Cooperation in Europe | Western European Union | Baltic Marine Environment Protection Commission | European Free Trade Association | European Patent Office | European Environmental Law | Institute of European Law", "content_hash": "993df2575105655ce875a4a905df23e74f30e4c4c0f44bf5ce05b0f1422ebec2", "domain": "law.cornell.edu", "fetched_at": "2026-10-09T05:17:56.509545+00:00", "language": ""}, {"id": "1383", "title": "All About Estimated Average Glucose", "url": "https://www.healthline.com/health/estimated-average-glucose", "final_url": "https://www.healthline.com/health/estimated-average-glucose", "text": "Your estimated average glucose (eAG) can help both you and your doctor understand your blood glucose levels over a 60- or 90-day period.If you have diabetes, you might have heard your doctor or healthcare professional mention estimated average glucose (eAG).But, what exactly is eAG, what does it tell you about your blood sugar levels, and why\u00e2\u0080\u0099s it important?This article will help answer these questions and explain why it\u00e2\u0080\u0099s helpful to know what your eAG is if you have diabetes. What is estimated average glucose?Estimated average glucose (eAG) refers to your average blood sugar (glucose) levels for the past 60 to 90 days. It can be reported in milligrams per deciliter (mg/dL) or millimoles per liter (mmol/L). To measure your eAG, you\u00e2\u0080\u0099ll need to take an A1C blood test.Unlike your daily blood glucose readings that measure your blood sugar level at that moment, eAG looks at the bigger picture. Your eAG measures your typical blood sugar level over the past few months.Knowing your eAG can help with the following aspects of diabetes management:diagnosing both prediabetes and diabetescorroborating daily blood glucose monitoring resultsdetermining whether your diabetes treatment plan is helpingmaking changes to your diabetes treatment plan reinforcing healthy lifestyle and diet choicesHow is it calculated?Once you know your A1C percentage, you can use the following chart to look up your eAG:A1C (%)eAG (mg/dL)eAG (mmol/L)4683.85975.461267.071548.6818310.1921211.81024013.41126914.91229816.5Alternatively, you can use this online calculator from the American Diabetes Association (ADA) to determine your eAG using your A1C and vice versa.There\u00e2\u0080\u0099s a direct relationship between A1C and eAG values, as represented by the following formula:eAG (mg/dL) = 28.7 x A1C \u00e2\u0080\u0093 46.7In general, both A1C and eAG readings provide the same information about your recent blood glucose levels, for instance, whether your treatment plan is helping. But eAG uses the same units (mg/dL or mmol/L) that you regularly see on your daily blood sugar readings, making it easier to interpret. Is there a target range for estimated average glucose?There\u00e2\u0080\u0099s no single eAG target for everyone. Your doctor will help you determine an appropriate target according to your age and circumstances. In addition, your target eAG may change over time.In general, most non-pregnant adults with diabetes are encouraged to maintain an eAG below 154 mg/dL (8.6 mmol/L). That corresponds to an A1C test result that\u00e2\u0080\u0099s less than 7 percent.The ADA defines the following eAG ranges:Normal range: less than 114 mg/dLPrediabetes range: greater than 114 mg/dL and less than 140 mg/dLDiabetes range: greater than 140 mg/dLAs indicated above, higher eAG values indicate higher average blood glucose levels over the past few months. If you\u00e2\u0080\u0099re in the prediabetes or diabetes range, you may be at an increased risk of developing diabetes complications.If your eAG value is higher than your target, it could be an opportunity to improve your personalized treatment plan. It\u00e2\u0080\u0099s important to talk with your doctor or healthcare professional about what you believe is working and what isn\u00e2\u0080\u0099t. Your doctor can then make changes to your plan as necessary. How often should you test your estimated average glucose?According to the 2021 diabetes care guidelines provided by the ADA, people with well-controlled diabetes should undergo an A1C blood test to determine their eAG at least twice per year.The same guidelines suggest testing at least 4 times per year for individuals whose treatment plan has recently changed, along with those who are having difficulty meeting their blood sugar targets.How does your estimated average glucose differ from daily monitoring?Daily glucose monitoring is another critical tool in diabetes management. But daily monitoring doesn\u00e2\u0080\u0099t give you a long-term view of your blood glucose levels.The readings on your monitor provide a snapshot of your blood sugar at a particular point in time. Your monitor might automatically calculate an average of all of these readings. But this number isn\u00e2\u0080\u0099t the same as your eAG.Your eAG accounts for your blood glucose levels 24 hours per day, including the times when you\u00e2\u0080\u0099re not likely to test. As the name suggests, it\u00e2\u0080\u0099s an estimate based on your A1C test results.If you have diabetes, you\u00e2\u0080\u0099re typically instructed to test your blood sugar when it\u00e2\u0080\u0099s low (for example, when you wake up, before a meal, or several hours after a meal). Your eAG is likely higher than the average on your monitor, providing a broader view of your blood sugar levels.The bottom lineYour eAG can help both you and your healthcare team understand your blood glucose levels over a 60- or 90-day period.To know your eAG, you\u00e2\u0080\u0099ll have to take an A1C blood test. Your eAG is reported in the same units that you typically see on your blood sugar meter, making it simple to interpret.Your doctor will help you determine an appropriate eAG target based on your age and additional factors. Depending on your eAG reading, your doctor may make changes to your diabetes treatment plan.", "content_hash": "1391a4f04a9a38e7fcb9fdc7259f22c8413d02d26f59c9c9c4de6f327d609e50", "domain": "healthline.com", "fetched_at": "2026-10-09T05:17:59.289263+00:00", "language": ""}, {"id": "1384", "title": "Keynote Address by the Managing Director/Chief Executive, Nigeria Deposit Insurance Corporation (NDIC), Mr. Thompson Oludare Sunday, on the Occasion of NDIC\u2019s Special Day at the 21st Abuja International Trade Fair, Wednesday, 30th September 2026", "url": "https://ndic.gov.ng/news-detail?slug=keynote-address-by-the-managing-director-chief-executive-nigeria-deposit-insurance-corporation-ndic-mr-thompson-oludare-sunday-on-the-occasion-of-ndic-s-special-day-at-the-21st-abuja-international-trade-fair-wednesday-30th-september-2026", "final_url": "https://ndic.gov.ng/news-detail?slug=keynote-address-by-the-managing-director-chief-executive-nigeria-deposit-insurance-corporation-ndic-mr-thompson-oludare-sunday-on-the-occasion-of-ndic-s-special-day-at-the-21st-abuja-international-trade-fair-wednesday-30th-september-2026", "text": "Keynote Address by the Managing Director/Chief Executive, Nigeria Deposit Insurance Corporation (NDIC), Mr. Thompson Oludare Sunday, on the Occasion of NDIC\u2019s Special Day at the 21st Abuja International Trade Fair, Wednesday, 30th September 2026 The theme of this year\u2019s Fair, \u201cResilience: Trade, Taxation and the Economy,\u201d is both timely and strategic. N NDIC September 30, 2026 5 min read It is my great pleasure to welcome our distinguished guests to the NDIC Special Day at the 21st Abuja International Trade Fair, organised by the Abuja Chamber of Commerce, Industry, Mines and Agriculture.Let me, at the outset, commend the Chamber for sustaining this important platform for 21 consecutive years.The theme of this year\u2019s Fair, \u201cResilience: Trade, Taxation and the Economy,\u201d is both timely and strategic. It speaks directly to the realities of an economy undergoing profound transformation through bold reforms aimed at building a stronger, more resilient and productive economic structure, in line with the Federal Government\u2019s vision of a $1 trillion economy by 2030.Distinguished guests, ladies and gentlemen, for over three decades, the Nigeria Deposit Insurance Corporation has remained a critical pillar of Nigeria\u2019s financial safety-net architecture. Through our mandate of deposit guarantee, bank supervision in collaboration with the Central Bank of Nigeria, failure resolution and bank liquidation, the NDIC has worked to ensure that the banking system remains safe, sound and resilient. These responsibilities have direct bearing on the resilience of the nation\u2019s economy as every thriving business needs a trusted financial system that can safeguard its working capital, facilitate payments and support access to credit for investment and expansion. This is where the NDIC comes in.To strengthen confidence in the banking system, the Corporation enhanced its deposit insurance coverage in 2024 with the maximum insured limit increased to \u20a65 million per depositor per Deposit Money Bank (DMB) and Mobile Money Operator (MMO), and \u20a62 million per depositor per Microfinance Bank (MFB), Primary Mortgage Bank (PMB) and Payment Service Bank (PSB). This significant enhancement provides full coverage for over 98 percent of depositors across the insured institutions, thereby protecting households, small businesses and other vulnerable depositors from the immediate consequences of bank failure.For depositors whose balances exceed the insured limits, the NDIC continues to pay liquidation dividends from recoveries realised through the recovery of debts owed to the failed institutions as well as the disposal of their physical assets. Our objective is straightforward: no depositor should lose confidence in the banking system merely because an insured institution has failed.We are also transforming the way depositors are reimbursed. Through the deployment of technology, including the Bank Verification Number (BVN), Single Customer View (SCV), NIBSS infrastructure and other digital solutions, the Corporation has moved from cumbersome, manual processes towards faster and seamless reimbursement. Today, verified depositors of failed banks receive their insured deposits within days of bank closure.Indeed, the NDIC has positioned itself to serve as not merely a payer of claims after bank failure, but as a Risk Minimizer. One that identifies vulnerabilities early, strengthens safeguards and works to prevent institutional problems from escalating into systemic crises.Accordingly, the Corporation has continued to re-set and strengthen its institutional framework in line with global best practices. These initiatives include the deployment of Risk-Based Supervision (RBS), an enhanced Differential Premium Assessment System (DPAS), the Single Customer View (SCV) Framework, a full Distress Resolution suites, and the Bank Liquidation Management System (BLMS), alongside stronger inter-agency collaboration, particularly with the Central Bank of Nigeria and other members of the financial safety net architecture.It is against this backdrop that I wish to seize this opportunity to make a simple but important appeal to Nigerians. Please, keep your money in licensed and regulated financial institutions.There are still Nigerians who keep substantial funds outside the formal banking system or entrust their savings to unlicensed fund managers, attracted by promises of extraordinary and unrealistic returns. Please note that the consequences of this can be devastating.The proliferation and collapse of Ponzi schemes have demonstrated, time and again, the enormous financial and emotional cost of placing hard-earned resources in unregulated schemes. If an investment promise sounds too good to be true, Nigerians should pause, ask questions and verify before committing their money.Still as part of our commitment to making depositor protection more accessible, transparent and convenient, the Corporation launched an upgraded website on 19 September 2026. I encourage us to visit the new www.ndic.gov.ng, which is designed as a one-stop digital gateway for depositors and other stakeholders. It prominently displays the enhanced deposit insurance coverage limits, incorporates automated features for claims processing, and provides an upgraded directory for checking institutions insured by the NDIC. It also features a one-click Quick Action Bar, giving users direct access to our four key services: File Claim; Check Banks; Report Failed Bank; and FAQs. In addition, the website incorporates an AI-powered virtual assistant to improve user interaction and provide faster access to information.This is more than a website upgrade. It is another step in our journey towards a more accessible, responsive and technology-driven NDIC. We encourage depositors, creditors and shareholders of closed banks to make full use of our digital platforms when processing their claims.Depositors can also help to accelerate reimbursement by ensuring that their account information is accurate and consistent across banks and properly linked to their BVNs. With the deployment of technology, depositors no longer need to endure the inconvenience of visiting failed bank premises or NDIC offices for routine physical verification or carrying large volumes of documents from one location to another.However, technology and regulation alone cannot guarantee financial security. Financial literacy is the first line of defence for every depositor and business owner.I therefore encourage businesses and members of the public to strengthen their financial literacy, embrace digital financial services responsibly, maintain sound financial practices and engage with relevant regulatory institutions whenever they require guidance. The more informed the public becomes, the stronger our collective capacity to build a resilient financial system.Distinguished guests, ladies and gentlemen, I invite you to visit the NDIC Pavilion, where our officials are on hand to provide information on deposit insurance, depositor protection, claims processing, financial literacy and other services of the Corporation.On behalf of the Board, Management and staff of the Nigeria Deposit Insurance Corporation, I congratulate the Executive and members of the Abuja Chamber of Commerce, Industry, Mines and Agriculture on the successful hosting of the 21st Abuja International Trade Fair. I also express our sincere appreciation to our revered Royal Fathers, distinguished guests, exhibitors, participants and members of the media for honouring our invitation and joining us on this special occasion.I thank you.Thompson Oludare SundayManaging Director/Chief Executive, NDICSeptember 30, 2026 All News Share More News October 2, 2026 NDIC Clinches Verbatim\u2019 s Pillar of Integrity in Public Service Award August 14, 2026 NDIC Reinforces Financial Safety Net Role Amid Banking Sector Reforms July 20, 2026 NDIC Hosts International Deposit Insurers' Africa Regional Meeting in Abuja July 7, 2026 SERVICOM Commends NDIC on Efficient Service Deliver to Stakeholders June 30, 2026 NDIC Highlights Expanded Deposit Protection and Accelerated Reimbursement Systems Browse all news", "content_hash": "1f4215d78c0f45e5cc52f21422484411207df8485abfd4072b883e5c7da7d897", "domain": "ndic.gov.ng", "fetched_at": "2026-10-09T05:18:01.374284+00:00", "language": ""}, {"id": "1385", "title": "What Is a Macro Calculator and How Do You Use It?", "url": "https://www.healthline.com/health/nutrition/macro-calculator", "final_url": "https://www.healthline.com/health/nutrition/macro-calculator", "text": "Discover how a macro calculator can help you achieve your health goals by providing customized recommendations for protein, carbs, and fats based on your unique needs and lifestyle.When it comes to health and fitness, one size rarely fits all. That\u00e2\u0080\u0099s why more people are turning to macro calculators: tools that provide customized nutritional recommendations for proteins, carbohydrates, and fats based on individual needs and lifestyle factors. Whether you\u00e2\u0080\u0099re aiming to lose fat, build muscle, or maintain your current weight, understanding and managing your macronutrient intake can be a game-changer in reaching your health goals.Understanding macro calculatorsA macro calculator is a tool designed to determine your ideal daily intake of macronutrients, the three major nutrients your body needs in large amounts:proteincarbohydratesfatsUnlike general diet advice, macro calculators tailor their recommendations based on your body composition, lifestyle, and fitness or weight loss goals. By doing so, they offer a more personalized and often more effective approach to nutrition.These calculators are rooted in science and use validated formulas to help users understand not just how many calories they need, but what those calories should be made of. That\u00e2\u0080\u0099s important because macronutrient balance affects everything from energy levels to muscle growth to fat loss.How macro calculators workMacro calculators use several key inputs to generate your unique nutritional blueprint:Age, height, and weight: These basic physical metrics help estimate your energy needs.Activity level: Ranges from sedentary to very active and adjusts your calorie and macro needs accordingly.Body composition: If you know your body fat percentage, it can refine the calculations even further.Fitness goals: Whether your aim is to lose weight, build muscle, or maintain your physique, the calculator adjusts your macro split to match.Using this information, the calculator estimates two core figures. One is your basal metabolic rate (BMR), which is the number of calories your body burns at rest. The other is your total daily energy expenditure (TDEE), which is the total number of calories you burn in a day, including all activities.From there, it distributes your calories into specific percentages of protein, carbs, and fat based on your goals.Benefits of tracking macronutrientsTracking macros offers several advantages. For instance, recent research found that tracking macros, regardless of the dietary pattern participants followed, led to modest weight loss and cardiovascular improvement within six months. Unlike calorie counting, macro tracking emphasizes nutritional quality, not just quantity. You\u00e2\u0080\u0099re not simply staying within a calorie limit; you\u00e2\u0080\u0099re ensuring your body gets the right balance of nutrients to function at its best.It\u00e2\u0080\u0099s also personalized. Macro calculators recognize that no two bodies are the same, and each person needs a unique mix of nutrients based on their metabolism, activity level, and goals.Most importantly, it\u00e2\u0080\u0099s goal-oriented. Whether you\u00e2\u0080\u0099re training as an athlete, building muscle, or simply aiming to improve overall health, tracking macros helps you align your diet with your specific objectives.How to use a macro calculator effectivelyTo make the most of a macro calculator, follow these steps:Be accurate with your inputs: The more precise the data you enter, the better the recommendations will be.Use the suggested values as a starting point: These aren\u00e2\u0080\u0099t set in stone, but they provide a strong foundation.Track your intake. Use a food tracking app to log your meals and compare them against your daily macro targets.Assess and adjust: After two to three weeks, evaluate how your body is responding. If you\u00e2\u0080\u0099re not seeing the desired results, tweak your macros accordingly.Recalculate as needed. As your weight, activity level, or goals change, so should your macro targets.Common macro distribution patternsWhile individual needs vary, common macro splits align with specific goals.Fat loss goals: Higher protein (30\u00e2\u0080\u009340%), moderate fat (20\u00e2\u0080\u009330%), lower carbs (30\u00e2\u0080\u009340%)Muscle gain goals: High protein (25\u00e2\u0080\u009335%), moderate fat (20\u00e2\u0080\u009330%), higher carbs (40\u00e2\u0080\u009355%)Maintenance goals: Balanced intake, about 30% protein, 30% fat, and 40% carbsThese templates can serve as useful starting points, but always be prepared to fine-tune based on your progress and personal preferences.TakeawayA macro calculator is a practical and personalized tool that can help you take control of your nutrition, support your fitness goals, and guide you toward a healthier, more balanced lifestyle.Although macro calculators are powerful tools, they\u00e2\u0080\u0099re just part of the equation. Food quality matters. A 2024 meta-analysis found that choosing healthy fats and non-processed protein sources, such as nuts, seeds, fish, and legumes, over refined carbs, saturated fats, and processed meats is linked to a lower overall risk of death.This highlights the importance of meeting your macro targets with nutrient-dense, whole foods.Also, keep in mind that everyone responds differently to various macro distributions, so some experimentation may be needed to find what works best for your body and goals.Above all, consistency matters most. The best nutrition plan isn\u00e2\u0080\u0099t necessarily the trendiest or even the most precise. It\u00e2\u0080\u0099s the one you can maintain over time.If you\u00e2\u0080\u0099re just getting started, consider working with a registered dietitian. They can provide personalized guidance, clear up common misconceptions, and help you develop a sustainable approach based on your unique physiology and lifestyle.", "content_hash": "538665e50e0c54dd015a3b65f875b8a6b36c8f1762001258184edc9174b6e00e", "domain": "healthline.com", "fetched_at": "2026-10-09T05:18:03.439129+00:00", "language": ""}, {"id": "1386", "title": "Historical Background on Elections Clause | U.S. Constitution Annotated | US Law | LII / Legal Information Institute", "url": "https://www.law.cornell.edu/constitution-conan/article-1/section-4/clause-1/historical-background-on-elections-clause", "final_url": "https://www.law.cornell.edu/constitution-conan/article-1/section-4/clause-1/historical-background-on-elections-clause", "text": "prev | next ArtI.S4.C1.1 Historical Background on Elections Clause Article I, Section 4, Clause 1: The Times, Places and Manner of holding Elections for Senators and Representatives, shall be prescribed in each State by the Legislature thereof; but the Congress may at any time by Law make or alter such Regulations, except as to the Places of chusing Senators. The Elections Clause gives state legislatures authority over Senate and House elections but allows Congress to regulate such elections and thereby override state election laws.1 FootnoteIn 1842, Congress passed its first legislation to regulate House and Senate elections by establishing the district system for House elections. Act of June 25, 1842, ch. 47, \u00a7 2, 5 Stat. 491. Later legislation provided that Representatives \u201cbe elected by districts composed of a compact and contiguous territory and containing as nearly as practicable an equal number of inhabitants.\u201d See, e.g., Act of Aug. 8, 1911, ch. 5, 37 Stat. 13. The only exception to Congress\u2019s authority over state elections\u2014 \u201cthe Places of chusing Senators\u201d \u2014became a nullity when the Seventeenth Amendment superseded Article I, Section 3, Clause 1, by providing for Senators to be elected by popular votes rather than selected by state legislatures.2 FootnoteU.S. Const. amend. XVII. Congress\u2019s authority to regulate elections did not extend to where state legislatures would choose the Senators, because, at that time, the choice of senators belonged solely to the state legislatures. See also Joseph Story, Commentaries on the Constitution of the United States \u00a7 826 (1833) ( \u201cThe choice is to be made by the state legislature; and it would not be either necessary, or becoming in congress to prescribe the place, where it should sit.\u201d ). How state and federal regulation of Senate and House elections interplay has been a topic of significant interest throughout the nation\u2019s history. During the Constitution\u2019s ratification, the proposal to allow Congress to set aside state laws for electing Senators and Representatives was controversial.3 FootnoteThe Federalist No. 59 (Alexander Hamilton) ( \u201cThis provision has not only been declaimed against by those who condemn the Constitution in the gross, but it has been censured by those who have objected with less latitude and greater moderation; and, in one instance it has been thought exceptionable by a gentleman who has declared himself the advocate of every other part of the system.\u201d ). In his Commentaries on the Constitution of the United States, Justice Joseph Story summarized state concerns that were raised during the ratification process. He stated: Congress might prescribe the times of election so unreasonably, as to prevent the attendance of the electors; or the place at so inconvenient a distance from the body of the electors, as to prevent a due exercise of the right of choice. And congress might contrive the manner of holding elections, so as to exclude all but their own favourites from office. They might modify the right of election as they please; they might regulate the number of votes by the quantity of property, without involving any repugnancy to the constitution.4 FootnoteJoseph Story, Commentaries on the Constitution of the United States \u00a7 814 (1833). In contrast to state concern over the ability of Congress to legislate how states would hold congressional elections, Alexander Hamilton, in the Federalist No. 59, reasoned that unless Congress had authority to regulate Senate and House elections, state legislatures might \u201cat any moment annihilate [the U.S. Government], by neglecting to provide for the choice of persons to administer its affairs.\u201d 5 FootnoteThe Federalist No. 59 (Alexander Hamilton). Noting that the Elections Clause gave state legislatures primary power over Senate and House elections, Hamilton took the position that Congress would likely involve itself in congressional elections only if \u201cextraordinary circumstances might render that interposition necessary to [the U.S. Government\u2019s] safety.\u201d 6 FootnoteId. Echoing Hamilton\u2019s expectation that only \u201cextraordinary circumstances\u201d would involve Congress in regulating House and Senate elections, Justice Story reasoned that, as representatives of states and their people, Members of Congress would be reluctant to impose election laws on objecting states.7 FootnoteSee Joseph Story, Commentaries on the Constitution of the United States \u00a7 818 (1833) ( \u201cWho are to pass the laws for regulating elections? The congress of the United States, composed of a senate chosen by the state legislatures, and of representatives chosen by the people of the states. Can it be imagined, that these persons will combine to defraud their constituents of their rights, or to overthrow the state authorities, or the state influence?\u201d ). Footnotes 1 In 1842, Congress passed its first legislation to regulate House and Senate elections by establishing the district system for House elections. Act of June 25, 1842, ch. 47, \u00a7 2, 5 Stat. 491. Later legislation provided that Representatives \u201cbe elected by districts composed of a compact and contiguous territory and containing as nearly as practicable an equal number of inhabitants.\u201d See, e.g., Act of Aug. 8, 1911, ch. 5, 37 Stat. 13. 2 U.S. Const. amend. XVII. Congress\u2019s authority to regulate elections did not extend to where state legislatures would choose the Senators, because, at that time, the choice of senators belonged solely to the state legislatures. See also Joseph Story, Commentaries on the Constitution of the United States \u00a7 826 (1833) ( \u201cThe choice is to be made by the state legislature; and it would not be either necessary, or becoming in congress to prescribe the place, where it should sit.\u201d ). 3 The Federalist No. 59 (Alexander Hamilton) ( \u201cThis provision has not only been declaimed against by those who condemn the Constitution in the gross, but it has been censured by those who have objected with less latitude and greater moderation; and, in one instance it has been thought exceptionable by a gentleman who has declared himself the advocate of every other part of the system.\u201d ). 4 Joseph Story, Commentaries on the Constitution of the United States \u00a7 814 (1833). 5 The Federalist No. 59 (Alexander Hamilton). 6 Id. 7 See Joseph Story, Commentaries on the Constitution of the United States \u00a7 818 (1833) ( \u201cWho are to pass the laws for regulating elections? The congress of the United States, composed of a senate chosen by the state legislatures, and of representatives chosen by the people of the states. Can it be imagined, that these persons will combine to defraud their constituents of their rights, or to overthrow the state authorities, or the state influence?\u201d ). U.S. Constitution Annotated Toolbox Explanation of the Constitution - from the Congressional Research Service", "content_hash": "280eab10ed528f815a4879a1dcdf4fdb7bfead83e6d565f52145f6f0f9e87f03", "domain": "law.cornell.edu", "fetched_at": "2026-10-09T05:18:04.689819+00:00", "language": ""}, {"id": "1387", "title": "NDIC Reinforces Financial Safety Net Role Amid Banking Sector Reforms", "url": "https://ndic.gov.ng/news-detail?slug=ndic-reinforces-financial-safety-net-role-amid-banking-sector-reforms", "final_url": "https://ndic.gov.ng/news-detail?slug=ndic-reinforces-financial-safety-net-role-amid-banking-sector-reforms", "text": "NDIC Reinforces Financial Safety Net Role Amid Banking Sector Reforms The Managing Director/Chief Executive of the Nigeria Deposit Insurance Corporation (NDIC), Mr. Thompson O. Sunday, delivered a goodwill address at the House Committee on Insurance & Actuarial Matters' Retreat in Lagos N NDIC August 14, 2026 1 min read The Managing Director/Chief Executive of the Nigeria Deposit Insurance Corporation (NDIC), Mr. Thompson O. Sunday, delivered a goodwill address at the House Committee on Insurance & Actuarial Matters' Retreat in Lagos, focusing on \"Strengthening the Financial Safety Net amid Banking Recapitalisation & Fintech Innovation.\"During the engagement, Mr. Sunday underscored the NDIC's critical position as a core component of Nigeria's financial safety net. The Corporation protects depositors and promotes financial system stability through its comprehensive deposit insurance scheme.The statistics presented are compelling: over 98 percent of depositors representing more than 281 million accounts across insured institutions are fully covered in the event of an insured financial institution's failure. This extensive coverage demonstrates the NDIC's commitment to safeguarding the interests of depositors nationwide.The address highlighted the importance of balancing traditional banking stability with emerging fintech innovations. As Nigeria's financial sector undergoes recapitalization efforts, the NDIC's role becomes increasingly vital in maintaining depositor confidence and system stability.The retreat provided a platform for dialogue between regulatory bodies and legislative oversight committees, ensuring coordinated efforts in strengthening Nigeria's financial architecture. By emphasizing the protective mechanisms in place, Mr. Sunday reinforced public trust in the banking system during a period of significant sector transformation.The NDIC's expanded insurance coverage and proactive engagement with stakeholders demonstrate its commitment to fostering a secure and resilient financial environment for all Nigerians. All News Share More News October 2, 2026 NDIC Clinches Verbatim\u2019 s Pillar of Integrity in Public Service Award September 30, 2026 Keynote Address by the Managing Director/Chief Executive, Nigeria Deposit Insurance Corporation (NDIC), Mr. Thompson Oludare Sunday, on the Occasion of NDIC\u2019s Special Day at the 21st Abuja International Trade Fair, Wednesday, 30th September 2026 July 20, 2026 NDIC Hosts International Deposit Insurers' Africa Regional Meeting in Abuja July 7, 2026 SERVICOM Commends NDIC on Efficient Service Deliver to Stakeholders June 30, 2026 NDIC Highlights Expanded Deposit Protection and Accelerated Reimbursement Systems Browse all news", "content_hash": "9bd2b468c53c76d06c56c982ad68159f89fb1ac9bd011440619e07ddd0d82c10", "domain": "ndic.gov.ng", "fetched_at": "2026-10-09T05:18:05.398114+00:00", "language": ""}, {"id": "1388", "title": "How to Calculate When You Should Go to Sleep", "url": "https://www.healthline.com/health/sleep/sleep-calculator", "final_url": "https://www.healthline.com/health/sleep/sleep-calculator", "text": "Your sleep needs vary during your lifetime. How many sleep cycles you need to feel rested can also vary.Keeping track of your sleep schedule might not always be your top priority, but getting enough good sleep is critical to your health.Knowing how much sleep you need to function at your best can help you determine what time to go to bed.This article is Day 2 of Healthline\u00e2\u0080\u0099s 3 Week Better Sleep Challenge. Follow along to discover small, meaningful habits to help you sleep better, feel more rested, and wake ready to thrive each day.Missed a day? Catch up with Day 1: Everything to Know About Your Circadian Rhythm, or continue on to Day 3: How to fix Your Sleep Schedule.How much sleep do\u00c2 you\u00c2 need?\u00c2 How much sleep you need changes throughout your lifetime. Sleep guidelines offer research-backed recommendations for the ideal amount of sleep for optimal health.The American Academy of Pediatrics and the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) provide these general guidelines for different age groups:Sleep guidelines by ageBirth to 3 months: 14 to 17 hours (including naps)4 to 11 months: 12 to 16 hours (including naps)1 to 2 years: 11 to 14 hours (including naps)3 to 5 years: 10 to 13 hours6 to 12 years: 9 to 12 hours13 to 18 years: 8 to 10 hours18 to 60 years: 7 or more hours61 to 64 years: 7 to 9 hours65 years and older: 7 to 8 hours Sleep needs can still vary depending on the person. What\u00e2\u0080\u0099s key is how you feel when you get various amounts of sleep.Here are a few questions to consider when evaluating your sleep needs:Do I feel rested after 7 hours of sleep, or do I need at least 8 or 9?Do I experience any daytime drowsiness?Do I rely on caffeine to keep me going throughout the day?Has my sleeping partner noticed me tossing and turning or having any sleep issues during the night? Sleep\u00c2 calculatorBedtimes are based on: your wake-up timecompleting five or six 90-minute sleep cyclesallowing 15 minutes to fall asleepWhat happens if you don\u00e2\u0080\u0099t get enough sleep?Too little sleep can affect your body\u00e2\u0080\u0099s systems and restorative functions.The occasional night of poor sleep generally won\u00e2\u0080\u0099t seriously impact your health. But, experts have linked ongoing sleep deprivation to serious health consequences, including a higher risk of chronic diseases and early death.Sleep deprivation can impact short-term and long-term physical, emotional, and cognitive health. \u00c2\u00bbMORE:10 Things That Happen to Your Body When You Lose SleepFrequently asked questionsHere, we answer common questions about sleep needs.Do your sleep needs change with age?Your need for sleep changes with age and typically stabilizes around age 20.As you get older, you generally need less sleep. Environmental, behavioral, and medical factors can influence how much sleep you need, and those may change throughout your life.Why am I still tired after sleeping for 8 hours?There are a few possible reasons you might wake up tired, even after sleeping for 8 hours. A good place to start exploring these reasons? Consider your sleep habits and sleep hygiene practices. If you wake up tired after sleeping for 8 hours, you may need to adjust your sleep hygiene practices or treat an underlying condition. Things that could detract from quality sleep include:your sleep environmentmovement or noise from a bed partner or petsleep disorders like insomnia or sleep apneachronic painan underlying medical or mental health conditionIs it healthy to nap during the day?Taking naps longer than 30 minutes during the day may negatively affect sleep quality at night.In a 2023 study, longer nappers who napped for longer than 30 minutes during the day had an increased risk for cardiovascular disease than people who napped for less than 30 minutes.\u00c2\u00bbMORE:7 Strategies to Help You Stay Awake During the DayThe bottom lineIf you aim for 7 to 9 hours of sleep each night, a sleep calculator like the one above can help you figure out what time to go to bed based on your wake-up time. Ideally, waking up at the end of your sleep cycle, when you\u00e2\u0080\u0099re most likely to feel rested, would be best. Quality sleep supports good health, so if you\u00e2\u0080\u0099re having trouble falling or staying asleep, consider contacting a healthcare professional. They can help you explore the underlying causes of sleep difficulties and offer guidance. You might also like:Do Women Need More Sleep Than Men?What Is Pink Noise and How Does It Compare with Other Sonic Hues?Key Ways to Revamp Your Sleep Schedule", "content_hash": "ba058d32a309407c6f8b3dcd14a1941f9bafcf20de02979343c773dbb8ce2632", "domain": "healthline.com", "fetched_at": "2026-10-09T05:18:07.420616+00:00", "language": ""}, {"id": "1389", "title": "Congress and Elections Clause | U.S. Constitution Annotated | US Law | LII / Legal Information Institute", "url": "https://www.law.cornell.edu/constitution-conan/article-1/section-4/clause-1/congress-and-elections-clause", "final_url": "https://www.law.cornell.edu/constitution-conan/article-1/section-4/clause-1/congress-and-elections-clause", "text": "prev | next ArtI.S4.C1.3 Congress and Elections Clause Article I, Section 4, Clause 1: The Times, Places and Manner of holding Elections for Senators and Representatives, shall be prescribed in each State by the Legislature thereof; but the Congress may at any time by Law make or alter such Regulations, except as to the Places of chusing Senators. Known as the Elections Clause, Article I, Section 4, Clause 1 provides for Congress and state legislatures to regulate the \u201cTimes, Places and Manner of holding elections for Senators and Representatives.\u201d 1 FootnoteU.S. Const. art. I, \u00a7 4, cl. 1. Under the Elections Clause, each state establishes how it will hold congressional elections, subject to Congress adopting or altering the state requirements (except as to the place of choosing Senators).2 FootnoteId. See Foster v. Love, 522 U.S. 67, 69 (1997) ( \u201c[I]t is well settled that the Elections Clause grants Congress \u2018the power to override state regulations\u2019 by establishing uniform rules for federal elections binding on the States.\u2019\u201d (quoting U.S. Term Limits, Inc. v. Thornton, 514 U.S. 779, 832\u201333 (1995))). The Elections Clause\u2019s \u201cTimes, Places and Manner\u201d encompasses \u201ca complete code for congressional elections, not only as to times and places, but in relation to notices, registration, supervision of voting, protection of voters, prevention of fraud and corrupt practices, counting of votes, duties of inspectors and canvassers, and making and publication of election returns.\u201d 3 FootnoteSmiley v. Holm, 285 U.S. 355, 366 (1932). States and Congress may also establish sanctions for violating election laws4 FootnoteId. at 369. and procedures for recounts5 FootnoteRoudebush v. Hartke, 405 U.S. 15, 24\u201325 (1972). and primaries.6 FootnoteUnited States v. Classic, 313 U.S. 299, 320 (1941). The Elections Clause however, does not permit states or Congress to set voter qualifications for congressional elections, which, under the Constitution, must be the same qualifications necessary to vote for the most numerous branch of the state legislature.7 FootnoteU.S. Const. art. I, \u00a7 2, cl. 1; U.S. Const. amend. XVII. See Arizona v. Inter Tribal Council of Ariz., Inc., 570 U.S. 1, 17 (2013) ( \u201cPrescribing voting qualifications, therefore, \u2018forms no part of the power to be conferred upon the national government\u2019 by the Elections Clause, which is \u2018expressly restricted to the regulation of the times, the places, and the manner of elections.\u2019\u201d (quoting The Federalist No. 60 (Alexander Hamilton))). See also Oregon v. Mitchell, 400 U.S. 112 (1970). Likewise, the Elections Clause does not allow states or Congress to change the qualifications to be a Member of the House of Representatives or the Senate, which are stipulated at Article I, Section 2, Clause 2 for the House and Article I, Section 3, Clause 3 for the Senate.8 FootnoteU.S. Const. art. I, \u00a7 2, cl. 2; U.S. Const. art. I, \u00a7 3, cl. 3. See United States Term Limits v. Thornton, 514 U.S. 779 (1995) By providing Congress power to preempt state election procedures, the Framers sought to prevent states from thwarting the Federal Government\u2019s operation by using state law to manipulate or preclude elections for the House of Representatives.9 FootnoteUnited States Term Limits v. Thornton, 514 U.S. 779, 808 (1995). For example, during the Constitutional Convention Gouverneur Morris of Pennsylvania expressed concern that \u201cthe States might make false returns and then make no provision for new elections,\u201d 10 Footnote2 The Records of the Federal Convention 241 (Max Farrand ed., 1901). while Alexander Hamilton observed in the Federalist Papers that \u201cNothing can be more evident than that an exclusive power of regulating elections for the national government, in the hands of the State legislatures, would leave the existence of the Union entirely at their mercy.\u201d 11 FootnoteThe Federalist No. 59 (Alexander Hamilton). See also Rucho v. Common Cause, No. 18\u2013422, slip op. at 9 (U.S. June 2019) (discussing Congress\u2019s authority under the Commerce Clause). Despite the Elections Clause providing Congress power to preempt state law governing elections, Congress did not exercise this power until 1842 when it passed a law requiring that Representatives be elected on a district basis.12 FootnoteAct of June 25, 1842, ch. 47, 5 Stat. 491. In 1870, Congress passed the first comprehensive federal statute to enforce the Fifteenth Amendment\u2019s guarantee against racial discrimination in voting. The Enforcement Act of 1870, ch. 114, 16 Stat. 140. Congress subsequently added contiguity, compactness, and substantial equality of population to districting requirements.13 FootnoteUnder the 1872 Act ( 17 Stat. 28), Congress provided for congressional districts to contain \u201cas nearly as practicable\u201d equal numbers of inhabitants. In 1901 ( 31 Stat. 733), Congress required districts to comprise \u201ccompact territory.\u201d In the Court\u2019s 1997 decision, Foster v. Love, the Supreme Court affirmed a lower court decision that, under the Elections Clause, federal law preempted a Louisiana statute governing congressional elections.14 FootnoteFoster v. Love, 522 U.S. 67 (1997). The Foster Court noted that while states can prescribe regulations governing the \u201cTimes, Places and Manner\u201d of holding elections, \u201cCongress may at any time by Law make or alter such Regulations.\u201d 15 FootnoteId. at 69. The Court stated: The [Elections] Clause is a default provision; it invests the States with responsibility for the mechanics of congressional elections but only so far as Congress declines to pre-empt state legislative choices. Thus, it is well settled that the Elections Clause grants Congress \u2018the power to override state regulations\u2019 by establishing uniform rules for federal elections, binding on the States. \u2018The regulations made by Congress are paramount to those made by the State legislature; and if they conflict therewith, the latter so far as the conflict extends, ceases to be operative.'16 FootnoteId. Under its Elections Clause authority, Congress has passed laws that govern how state election systems may operate.17 FootnoteRucho v. Common Clause, No. 18\u2013422, slip op. at 30\u201334 (U.S. June 2019). For example, in Arizona v. Inter Tribal Council of Arizona, the Court held that the National Voter Registration Act of 1993, which required states to use a specific federal form to register voters for federal elections, preempted an Arizona law that imposed an additional evidence-of-citizenship requirement.18 Footnote570 U.S. 1 (2013). Unlike the Arizona law, which required documentary evidence of citizenship, the federal form required only that an applicant wishing to vote in federal elections to swear under penalty of perjury that he or she was a citizen. Id. at 5. The Arizona Court further noted that state authority to regulate congressional elections is less than its general police powers because the Constitution provides expressly for state law governing elections to be preempted by federal law. The Court stated: \u201cUnlike the States\u2019 \u2018historic police powers,\u2019 the States\u2019 role in regulating congressional elections\u2014while weighty and worthy of respect\u2014has always existed subject to the express qualification that \u2018it terminates according to federal law.\u2019\u201d 19 FootnoteId. (quoting Rice v. Santa Fe Elevator Corp., 331 U.S. 218, 230 (1947); Buckman Co. v. Plaintiffs\u2019 Legal Comm., 531 U.S. 341, 347 (2001)). The Court has also held that where a primary election is an integral part of choosing a Member of Congress, the right to vote in that primary election is subject to congressional protection20 FootnoteUnited States v. Classic, 313 U.S. 299, 315\u2013321 (1941). The authority of Newberry v. United States, 256 U.S. 232 (1921), to the contrary has been vitiated. Cf. United States v. Wurzbach, 280 U.S. 396 (1930). and includes the opportunity to cast a ballot and to have it counted honestly.21 FootnoteUnited States v. Mosley, 238 U.S. 383 (1915); United States v. Saylor, 322 U.S. 385, 387 (1944). Congress may secure elections from personal violence and intimidation as well as from failures to count ballots lawfully cast22 FootnoteEx parte Yarbrough, 110 U.S. 651 (1884); United States v. Mosley, 238 U.S. 383 (1915) or the stuffing of ballot boxes with fraudulent ballots.23 FootnoteUnited States v. Saylor, 322 U.S. 385 (1944) Congress may also enforce election laws by imposing sanctions24 FootnoteEx parte Siebold, 100 U.S. 371, 392 (1880) (holding that Congress\u2019s power under the Elections Clause \u201cis paramount, and may be exercised at any time, and to any extent which it deems expedient; and so far as it is exercised, and no farther, the regulations effected supersede those of the State which are inconsistent therewith.\u201d ); Ex parte Clarke, 100 U.S. 399 (1880); United States v. Gale, 109 U.S. 65 (1883); In re Coy, 127 U.S. 731 (1888). or punish state election officers for violating legal duties relating to congressional elections.25 FootnoteEx parte Siebold, 100 U.S. 371, 396\u201397 (1880). But the Court has held that bribing voters, although within Congress\u2019s power under other clauses of the Constitution, does not implicate the Elections Clause.26 FootnoteUnited States v. Bathgate, 246 U.S. 220, 225\u201326 (1918); United States v. Gradwell, 243 U.S. 476, 485 (1917) ( \u201c[T]he policy of Congress for [a] great . . . part of our constitutional life has been . . . to leave the conduct of the election of its members to state laws, administered by state officers, and that whenever it has assumed to regulate such elections it has done so by positive and clear statutes.\u201d ). Finally, the Court has recognized that because the Elections Clause specifically vests Congress and the states with authority over the \u201cTime, Places and Manner\u201d of congressional elections, the Court\u2019s authority over such matters is limited.27 FootnoteSee, e.g., Husted v. A. Philip Randolph Inst., No. 16-960, slip op. at 25\u201326 (U.S. June 2018) ( \u201cWe have no authority to dismiss the considered judgment of Congress and the Ohio Legislature regarding the probative value of a registrant\u2019s failure to send back a [voter verification] return card.\u201d ). Footnotes 1 U.S. Const. art. I, \u00a7 4, cl. 1. 2 Id. See Foster v. Love, 522 U.S. 67, 69 (1997) ( \u201c[I]t is well settled that the Elections Clause grants Congress \u2018the power to override state regulations\u2019 by establishing uniform rules for federal elections binding on the States.\u2019\u201d (quoting U.S. Term Limits, Inc. v. Thornton, 514 U.S. 779, 832\u201333 (1995))). 3 Smiley v. Holm, 285 U.S. 355, 366 (1932). 4 Id. at 369. 5 Roudebush v. Hartke, 405 U.S. 15, 24\u201325 (1972). 6 United States v. Classic, 313 U.S. 299, 320 (1941). 7 U.S. Const. art. I, \u00a7 2, cl. 1; U.S. Const. amend. XVII. See Arizona v. Inter Tribal Council of Ariz., Inc., 570 U.S. 1, 17 (2013) ( \u201cPrescribing voting qualifications, therefore, \u2018forms no part of the power to be conferred upon the national government\u2019 by the Elections Clause, which is \u2018expressly restricted to the regulation of the times, the places, and the manner of elections.\u2019\u201d (quoting The Federalist No. 60 (Alexander Hamilton))). See also Oregon v. Mitchell, 400 U.S. 112 (1970). 8 U.S. Const. art. I, \u00a7 2, cl. 2; U.S. Const. art. I, \u00a7 3, cl. 3. See United States Term Limits v. Thornton, 514 U.S. 779 (1995) 9 United States Term Limits v. Thornton, 514 U.S. 779, 808 (1995). 10 2 The Records of the Federal Convention 241 (Max Farrand ed., 1901). 11 The Federalist No. 59 (Alexander Hamilton). See also Rucho v. Common Cause, No. 18\u2013422, slip op. at 9 (U.S. June 2019) (discussing Congress\u2019s authority under the Commerce Clause). 12 Act of June 25, 1842, ch. 47, 5 Stat. 491. In 1870, Congress passed the first comprehensive federal statute to enforce the Fifteenth Amendment\u2019s guarantee against racial discrimination in voting. The Enforcement Act of 1870, ch. 114, 16 Stat. 140. 13 Under the 1872 Act ( 17 Stat. 28), Congress provided for congressional districts to contain \u201cas nearly as practicable\u201d equal numbers of inhabitants. In 1901 ( 31 Stat. 733), Congress required districts to comprise \u201ccompact territory.\u201d 14 Foster v. Love, 522 U.S. 67 (1997). 15 Id. at 69. 16 Id. 17 Rucho v. Common Clause, No. 18\u2013422, slip op. at 30\u201334 (U.S. June 2019). 18 570 U.S. 1 (2013). Unlike the Arizona law, which required documentary evidence of citizenship, the federal form required only that an applicant wishing to vote in federal elections to swear under penalty of perjury that he or she was a citizen. Id. at 5. 19 Id. (quoting Rice v. Santa Fe Elevator Corp., 331 U.S. 218, 230 (1947); Buckman Co. v. Plaintiffs\u2019 Legal Comm., 531 U.S. 341, 347 (2001)). 20 United States v. Classic, 313 U.S. 299, 315\u2013321 (1941). The authority of Newberry v. United States, 256 U.S. 232 (1921), to the contrary has been vitiated. Cf. United States v. Wurzbach, 280 U.S. 396 (1930). 21 United States v. Mosley, 238 U.S. 383 (1915); United States v. Saylor, 322 U.S. 385, 387 (1944). 22 Ex parte Yarbrough, 110 U.S. 651 (1884); United States v. Mosley, 238 U.S. 383 (1915) 23 United States v. Saylor, 322 U.S. 385 (1944) 24 Ex parte Siebold, 100 U.S. 371, 392 (1880) (holding that Congress\u2019s power under the Elections Clause \u201cis paramount, and may be exercised at any time, and to any extent which it deems expedient; and so far as it is exercised, and no farther, the regulations effected supersede those of the State which are inconsistent therewith.\u201d ); Ex parte Clarke, 100 U.S. 399 (1880); United States v. Gale, 109 U.S. 65 (1883); In re Coy, 127 U.S. 731 (1888). 25 Ex parte Siebold, 100 U.S. 371, 396\u201397 (1880). 26 United States v. Bathgate, 246 U.S. 220, 225\u201326 (1918); United States v. Gradwell, 243 U.S. 476, 485 (1917) ( \u201c[T]he policy of Congress for [a] great . . . part of our constitutional life has been . . . to leave the conduct of the election of its members to state laws, administered by state officers, and that whenever it has assumed to regulate such elections it has done so by positive and clear statutes.\u201d ). 27 See, e.g., Husted v. A. Philip Randolph Inst., No. 16-960, slip op. at 25\u201326 (U.S. June 2018) ( \u201cWe have no authority to dismiss the considered judgment of Congress and the Ohio Legislature regarding the probative value of a registrant\u2019s failure to send back a [voter verification] return card.\u201d ). U.S. Constitution Annotated Toolbox Explanation of the Constitution - from the Congressional Research Service", "content_hash": "a81b6a2b54096cb054659f83eef6d72de1bd00be9322d67bc86c60275cf56721", "domain": "law.cornell.edu", "fetched_at": "2026-10-09T05:18:08.898696+00:00", "language": ""}, {"id": "1390", "title": "Health News", "url": "https://www.healthline.com/health-news", "final_url": "https://www.healthline.com/health-news", "text": "Health NewsHealthline News reports on emerging research, new treatments, diet, exercise, and trending topics in health and wellness. All articles are written by our network of editors and contributors. Everything we publish is fact checked by our panel of experts.October 8, 2026Taking Blood Pressure Meds? DASH Diet Likely Safe for Potassium LevelsA new study reports that the DASH diet doesn't raise potassium levels in people taking high blood pressure medication for hypertension. Researchers\u00e2\u0080\u00a6READ MOREOctober 7, 2026Pain Prevalence Higher in Women, Increases Most Rapidly Before 55A large global study of more than 6 million people reveals striking differences in how women and men experience pain and how pain changes across the\u00e2\u0080\u00a6READ MOREOctober 7, 2026Detached Nails More Than 4 Times Higher Among GLP-1 Users, Study FindsIn a new study, GLP-1 users reported nail detachment more than four times as often as nonusers, and nail discoloration affected 46% of GLP-1 users. A\u00e2\u0080\u00a6READ MOREOctober 6, 2026Switching from GLP-1 Injections to Wegovy Pill Shows Additional Weight Loss of 4.1%People who switched from an injectable GLP-1 weight loss medication to the Wegovy pill continued to lose weight over the next 3 months, a new study\u00e2\u0080\u00a6READ MOREOctober 6, 2026Oral GLP-1 Orforglipron (Foundayo) Achieves Weight Loss for Women of All AgesA new analysis found that the diabetes medication orforglipron (Foundayo), an oral GLP-1, helped women lose up to 14% of their body weight, regardless\u00e2\u0080\u00a6READ MOREOctober 5, 2026Increasing Ozempic Dose Linked to Lower Heart Risk Than Switching to MounjaroRecent research suggests that increasing the dose of Ozempic may be associated with a lower risk of major heart events than switching to Mounjaro.READ MOREOctober 4, 2026Can the BPC-157 \u00e2\u0080\u0098Wolverine\u00e2\u0080\u0099 Peptide Really Help With Pain and Recovery?Proponents of BPC-157, the \u00e2\u0080\u009cWolverine\u00e2\u0080\u009d peptide, tout injury recovery and pain relief despite limited human evidence to establish safety and\u00e2\u0080\u00a6READ MOREOctober 3, 2026Cutting Back on Ultra-Processed Foods May Help Ease Depression SymptomsA small study suggests that cutting back on ultra-processed foods may help ease symptoms of depression.READ MOREOctober 2, 2026FDA Issues Class II Blood Pressure Medication Recall, Over 13,000 Bottles AffectedThe FDA has issued a recall of 13,567 bottles of 25 mg chlorthalidone tablets, which had failed lab tests. It's the manufacturer\u00e2\u0080\u0099s second recall of\u00e2\u0080\u00a6READ MOREOctober 2, 2026Letter From the Editor-in-Chief: Is Worrying About Sleep Keeping Us Awake?A study analyzing more than 95,000 people's sleep data found REM and deep sleep lowers risk of 80+ diseases. Healthline's Robin Hough shares why he's\u00e2\u0080\u00a6READ MOREOctober 1, 2026Low-Energy Diet Plus Structured Exercise Reverses Early Onset DiabetesA new study found that a low-energy diet combined with a structured exercise program reversed type 2 diabetes in more than half of the participants.READ MOREOctober 1, 2026Wall Squats, Planks Once a Week May Help Lower Your Blood PressureJust one or two isometric exercise sessions per week may help lower blood pressure in the short term, according to a new study. Performing isometric\u00e2\u0080\u00a6READ MORESeptember 30, 2026Insomnia Isn\u00e2\u0080\u0099t Just Exhausting \u00e2\u0080\u0094 It May Raise Your Risk of Stroke, DementiaA recent review found that insomnia may be linked to a higher risk of stroke, dementia, and other health issues.READ MORESeptember 30, 20268-Hour Time-Restricted Eating Window May Lead to Greater Weight LossA recent study found that shorter time-restricted eating windows may lead to modestly greater weight loss. However, experts note that what you eat may\u00e2\u0080\u00a6READ MORESeptember 29, 2026Fullspan Health Announces First-Ever 2026 Healthline AwardsWinners of the inaugural 2026 Healthline Awards have been announced by Fullspan Health, a B2B healthcare platform. The Healthline Awards feature more\u00e2\u0080\u00a6READ MORESeptember 29, 2026Simone Biles Thought Her Itchy, Flaky Scalp Was Dandruff. It Was EczemaOlympic gold medalist Simone Biles says her eczema symptoms had her feeling \u00e2\u0080\u009cashamed and embarrassed\u00e2\u0080\u009d before diagnosis. Here\u00e2\u0080\u0099s how she keeps flare-ups\u00e2\u0080\u00a6READ MORESeptember 28, 2026Ultra-Processed Foods Linked to Higher Risk of Multiple Diseases, DeathA large study has found that higher ultra-processed food (UPF) intake was linked to a greater risk of multiple diseases, including type 2 diabetes\u00e2\u0080\u00a6READ MORESeptember 25, 2026NBA Star Steph Curry Didn\u00e2\u0080\u0099t Realize His Blurry Vision Was a Sign of an Eye DiseaseNBA star Stephen Curry says he had grown accustomed to blurry vision before being diagnosed with keratoconus. Here are the early signs and symptoms he\u00e2\u0080\u00a6READ MORESeptember 24, 2026Women With Enlarged Breasts More Likely to Experience Migraine, Sleep ApneaMacromastia, or enlarged breast tissue, is linked to a higher risk of chronic migraine, neck pain, and sleep apnea in women, according to a new study\u00e2\u0080\u00a6READ MORESeptember 23, 2026FDA Approves Lilly\u00e2\u0080\u0099s New Oral Combo Pill for Common Breast Cancer MutationThe FDA approved Eli Lilly's new oral combo drug for a common breast cancer mutation. The treatment includes Inluriyo (imlunestrant) taken with\u00e2\u0080\u00a6READ MORESeptember 22, 2026Testosterone Prescriptions for Women Surge Amid Pharmacy Access BarriersTestosterone prescriptions for females have surged 146% since 2023. While clinical guidance supports its use for HSDD after menopause, evidence for\u00e2\u0080\u00a6READ MORESeptember 20, 2026Think You\u00e2\u0080\u0099re Too Young to Worry About Alzheimer\u00e2\u0080\u0099s? Maria Shriver Says Think AgainJournalist and author Maria Shriver wants people, particularly women, to start thinking about Alzheimer\u00e2\u0080\u0099s risk long before symptoms appear. Here\u00e2\u0080\u0099s why\u00e2\u0080\u00a6READ MORESeptember 19, 2026Aerobic Exercise May Improve Brain Health in Postmenopausal WomenA new study found that regular aerobic exercise may help improve executive function in postmenopausal females, highlighting an effective strategy for\u00e2\u0080\u00a6READ MORESeptember 18, 2026Microdosing GLP-1s Like Wegovy, Zepbound May Still Lead to Weight LossRecent research suggests that \u00e2\u0080\u009cmicrodosing\u00e2\u0080\u009d GLP-1 medications may still lead to weight loss. However, lower doses were associated with less weight\u00e2\u0080\u00a6READ MORESeptember 17, 2026Women, Young Adults Experience Fatigue More Often, Says CDCA recent CDC report shows that the majority of U.S. adults experience fatigue at least some of the time, with females and young adults more likely to\u00e2\u0080\u00a6READ MORESeptember 17, 2026Are You Eating Enough Whole Grains to Lower Your Heart Disease Risk?Most people aren't getting enough whole grains in their diets. A large meta-analysis suggests that 4 to 6 servings of whole grains per day offers\u00e2\u0080\u00a6READ MORESeptember 16, 2026Is Diet Quality More Important For Weight Loss Than Counting Calories?Counting calories isn\u00e2\u0080\u0099t the only factor that matters for weight loss. A two-year study found that a Mediterranean-style diet produced similar weight\u00e2\u0080\u00a6READ MORESeptember 15, 2026Love to Read? Your Book Habit May Lower Alzheimer\u00e2\u0080\u0099s Risk by 33%Reading for pleasure may do more than entertain you. Research suggests it can support mental well-being and brain health from childhood through older\u00e2\u0080\u00a6READ MORESeptember 15, 2026Most Men Unaware That Dairy Intake Is Linked to Prostate Cancer RiskA new survey found that only 1 in 5 men in the United States is aware of the potential link between dairy product consumption and prostate cancer\u00e2\u0080\u00a6READ MORESeptember 14, 2026Why Your Morning Coffee May Boost Your Mood Before You Even Take a SipA small recent study suggests that smelling coffee for just five minutes may help boost your mood and improve your alertness.READ MORESeptember 13, 2026\u00e2\u0080\u0098The Pitt\u00e2\u0080\u0099 Star Katherine LaNasa Says Early Breast Cancer Detection Saved Her LifeActor Katherine LaNasa, best known for her role as nurse Dana Evans on \"The Pitt,\" opens up about her surprising breast cancer diagnosis and how early\u00e2\u0080\u00a6READ MORESeptember 12, 2026Her Bloating Turned Out to Be Colon Cancer at 37. Then She Learned She Had a BRCA2 MutationAfter being diagnosed with stage 1 colon cancer at 37, Marisa Stachelski learned she had a BRCA2 mutation that increased her risk of several other\u00e2\u0080\u00a6READ MORESeptember 11, 2026Love Your Coffee Piping Hot? It May Raise Your Esophageal Cancer RiskDrinking coffee or tea at very high temperatures could triple your risk of esophageal cancer, researchers say. Allowing hot beverages to cool before\u00e2\u0080\u00a6READ MORESeptember 10, 2026Even Low Light Exposure During Sleep May Raise Your Risk of Heart Failure, AFibNew research shows that even dim light during sleep \u00e2\u0080\u0094 as low as 3 lux \u00e2\u0080\u0094 may thicken heart muscle walls and reduce heart flexibility, raising\u00e2\u0080\u00a6READ MORESeptember 10, 2026Nearly 1 in 3 Women in Their 20s Have Never Had a Cervical ScreeningNearly 1 in 3 women ages 21 to 29 have never been screened for cervical cancer, according to new research. Regular screening can detect precancerous\u00e2\u0080\u00a6READ MORESeptember 9, 2026Healthy Diet and Fitness Program May Improve Prostate Cancer OutcomesAn enhanced diet and exercise program may support men during prostate cancer treatment, according to a new study. Men undergoing androgen deprivation\u00e2\u0080\u00a6READ MORESeptember 9, 2026Planning a K-Beauty Trip? What a Top Aesthetic Doctor Says to Look for Before BookingCurious about K-beauty and \"glass skin\" treatments? Expert Dr. Heewon Suh, MD, shares key advice on finding credentialed doctors, safety, and\u00e2\u0080\u00a6READ MORESeptember 8, 2026U.S. Medical Groups Issue Flu, COVID Vaccine Guidance Independent of CDCFour medical groups released independent guidance for the upcoming 2026\u00e2\u0080\u00932027 respiratory virus season, each recommending a flu shot for everyone 6\u00e2\u0080\u00a6READ MORESeptember 8, 2026Drinking Alcohol Raises Cancer Risk. Is Any Amount Safe for Health?Three recent studies highlight the health risks associated with alcohol, linking consumption to cancer deaths and several health conditions, including\u00e2\u0080\u00a6READ MORESeptember 7, 2026FDA Approves 4 Updated COVID-19 Vaccines for 2026\u00e2\u0080\u00932027 SeasonThe FDA has approved four updated COVID-19 vaccines for the 2026\u00e2\u0080\u00932027 season as cases rise across much of the United States. Experts explain who\u00e2\u0080\u00a6READ MORE", "content_hash": "c8a47aeae2bfe86e2474a8fb70e5e4aafe6cf87fb5dd85bfbf39442330d40545", "domain": "healthline.com", "fetched_at": "2026-10-09T05:18:12.113501+00:00", "language": ""}, {"id": "1391", "title": "MD/CE NDIC\u2019S Welcome Remark at IADI-ARC Workshop & Annual Meeting 2026", "url": "https://ndic.gov.ng/article?slug=md-ce-ndic-s-welcome-remark-at-iadi-arc-workshop-annual-meeting-2026", "final_url": "https://ndic.gov.ng/article?slug=md-ce-ndic-s-welcome-remark-at-iadi-arc-workshop-annual-meeting-2026", "text": "MD/CE NDIC\u2019S Welcome Remark at IADI-ARC Workshop & Annual Meeting 2026 This year\u2019s theme, \u201cSafeguarding Stability: Public Awareness and Crisis Readiness for a Stronger Future,\u201d N NDIC July 21, 2026 6 min read The Chairman, Senate Committee on Banking, Insurance, and Other Financial InstitutionsThe Chairman, House Committee on Banking, Insurance, and Other Financial InstitutionsDistinguished Members of the National AssemblyThe Honourable Minister of Finance and Coordinating Minister of the EconomyThe Governor of the Central Bank of NigeriaThe Secretary General, International Association of Deposit InsurersThe Chairperson of the International Association of Deposit Insurers, Africa Regional CommitteeHeads of Deposit Insurance OrganisationsOther representatives of Africa Regional Committee membersRepresentatives of other Financial Services Regulation Coordinating Committee membersDistinguished Speakers, Moderators and FacilitatorsLadies and Gentlemen, Good MorningIt is with immense pleasure and honour that I welcome you all to Abuja, the Federal Capital Territory of the Federal Republic of Nigeria for the 2026 International Association of Deposit Insurers (IADI) Africa Regional Committee Annual Meeting and Workshop.On behalf of the Board, Management and Staff of the Nigeria Deposit Insurance Corporation (NDIC), I extend our warmest welcome and sincere appreciation to all delegates who have travelled from across Africa and other regions of the world to participate in this important gathering.Your presence here today reflects our shared commitment to strengthening depositor protection, preserving public confidence in financial institutions and promoting effective deposit insurance systems. These objectives remain central to the mandates of the NDIC and to the mission of deposit insurers world over.This year\u2019s theme, \u201cSafeguarding Stability: Public Awareness and Crisis Readiness for a Stronger Future,\u201d is timely and highly relevant. Across the globe, deposit insurance systems are having to adapt to a rapidly changing financial environment shaped by digital innovation, fintech developments, artificial intelligence and increasing cross-border financial activities.While these developments present significant opportunities to expand financial inclusion and improve service delivery, they also create new risks that require proactive, coordinated responses from financial safety-net participants.Distinguished ladies and gentlemen, confidence remains the most valuable asset in any financial system. Indeed, trust takes years to build but can be eroded within days if stakeholders perceive uncertainty or instability. As deposit insurers, central banks, supervisors, and resolution authorities, our collective responsibility extends beyond establishing protective frameworks; it includes ensuring that the public understands, trusts, and relies on those frameworks during both normal and turbulent times.Public awareness is, therefore, an essential pillar of effective deposit insurance systems, and a critical tool for deposit insurers. A well-informed depositor is more likely to make rational decisions and less likely to react adversely to rumours, misinformation, during periods of uncertainty. Public awareness must therefore be treated not as a peripheral concern but as a pillar of stability. This underscores the importance of the discussions we will have during this Workshop on building confidence in normal times and strengthening communication strategies during crises.Equally important is robust crisis preparedness. The lessons from the 2023 banking turmoil, the most severe episode of systemic stress since the 2007 \u2013 2008 Global Financial Crisis, underscore the need for readiness in safeguarding financial stability. Although different factors drove the bank failures, they triggered a widespread crisis of confidence in banks and financial markets, prompting national and international policymakers to examine the underlying causes and draw critical lessons.These events serve as a reminder that financial crises can emerge unexpectedly and evolve rapidly, particularly in digital environments, where information travels instantaneously, and depositor reactions can be amplified by technology and social media. In such circumstances, preparedness, effective coordination and timely intervention can make the difference between maintaining stability and allowing contagion to spread throughout the financial system.It is therefore befitting that this Workshop will examine practical approaches to crisis readiness, institutional preparedness, coordinated responses among financial safety-net participants and strategies for building resilience within our respective jurisdictions. The sessions and roundtable discussions have been carefully designed to facilitate the exchange of experiences, lessons learned and innovative solutions to common challenges.Distinguished delegates, Nigeria remains committed to strengthening the resilience of its financial system through sound regulation, effective supervision, and robust depositor protection arrangements. The NDIC, working closely with the Federal Ministry of Finance, the Central Bank of Nigeria, and other safety-net participants, continues to play a critical role in maintaining financial stability and protecting depositors.Since its establishment in 1988 and commencement of its operations in 1989, the NDIC has continuously adapted its policies, tools and operational frameworks to respond to the changing realities of Nigeria\u2019s financial sector. Today, the Corporation remains focused on strengthening its operational readiness through sustained capacity building, enhanced depositor reimbursement systems, intensified public awareness initiatives, and improved crisis management capabilities, all aligned with international best practices.Distinguished delegates, Deposit Insurance Systems across Africa have recorded remarkable progress and transformation over the years, significantly enhancing their operational effectiveness and resilience. These achievements have strengthened the capacity of our institutions to protect depositors, promote confidence in the financial system and respond more effectively to emerging challenges.The Africa Regional Committee (ARC) has evolved into an invaluable platform for peer learning, technical cooperation, capacity building and policy dialogue, through which member institutions have exchanged experiences and shared best practices. This growing collaboration reflects our collective recognition that the challenges confronting our financial systems are increasingly interconnected and require coordinated responses, sustained knowledge sharing and robust cooperation among deposit insurers across the continent.As our financial systems become more interconnected, no institution can effectively address emerging risks in isolation. The challenges before us require stronger partnerships, greater information sharing, enhanced cross-border cooperation and continuous investment in institutional capacity. This Annual Meeting and Workshop provides an excellent opportunity to advance these objectives.Over the next few days, I encourage all participants to actively engage, share experiences, and contribute practical insights from their respective jurisdictions. The issues before us are increasingly interconnected, and require collective responses.I am confident that the deliberations during this Workshop will generate valuable ideas and practical recommendations to strengthen public awareness, enhance crisis preparedness, and improve depositor protection across all our regions.The NDIC remains committed to working closely with IADI, the Africa Regional Committee and our fellow deposit insurers to advance international best practices and strengthen financial safety nets for the benefit of all the stakeholders we serve.I wish to express our sincere appreciation to the International Association of Deposit Insurers, the Africa Regional Committee, our distinguished speakers, moderators, facilitators and delegates for honouring our invitation. We are equally grateful to the Federal Government of Nigeria, the Federal Ministry of Finance, the Central Bank of Nigeria and all the members of the Financial Services Regulation Coordinating Committee for their continued support and collaboration.Once again, I welcome you warmly to Abuja, Nigeria. I wish you fruitful deliberations, a rewarding experience and a successful Workshop.Thank you. All Articles Share More Articles July 22, 2026 Hon. Minister of Finance's Keynote Speech at IADI-ARC Workshop 2026 July 17, 2026 NDIC Hosts IADI Africa Regional Committee Meeting 2026 July 1, 2026 Update on the Revocation of the Operating Licenses of 46 Micro-Finance Banks by the Central Bank of Nigeria June 19, 2026 Disposal of Landed Properties and Other Physical Assets, Defunct Primary Mortgage Banks June 15, 2026 Sale of Chattels of Closed Primary Mortgage Banks by Public Auction and Public Competitive Bid. Browse all articles", "content_hash": "d8d4a8c067a22aca16f202737a3a12194ad04dae30108e4283540c3af0c8fece", "domain": "ndic.gov.ng", "fetched_at": "2026-10-09T05:18:14.921141+00:00", "language": ""}, {"id": "1392", "title": "Newsletters", "url": "https://www.healthline.com/newsletter-signup", "final_url": "https://www.healthline.com/newsletter-signup", "text": "NewslettersGive your inbox a healthy dose of well-being1. Select all the newsletters you\u00e2\u0080\u0099d like to receiveThree Times WeeklyWellness WireGet expert advice on trending health topics, and hear how our editors practice wellness in their everyday lives.Get expert advice on trending health topics, and hear how our editors practice wellness in their everyday lives.Three Times WeeklyNutritionOur team of registered dietitians answers life's big nutrition questions \u00e2\u0080\u0094 plus, get a delicious new recipe every week!Our team of registered dietitians answers life's big nutrition questions \u00e2\u0080\u0094 plus, get a delicious new recipe every week!Twice MonthlyWishlistWe vet and review hundreds of wellness brands, services, and products to help you live better.We vet and review hundreds of wellness brands, services, and products to help you live better.DailyDaily NewsStay on top of the latest health and wellness news with this daily dose of stories.Stay on top of the latest health and wellness news with this daily dose of stories.WeeklyType 1 DiabetesWe'll send you tips on eating wisely, news on breakthroughs, and more resources to help you stay on top of your health.We'll send you tips on eating wisely, news on breakthroughs, and more resources to help you stay on top of your health.Twice WeeklyType 2 DiabetesLearn how to manage your diet, stay on top of your blood sugar levels, and better understand type 2 diabetes.Learn how to manage your diet, stay on top of your blood sugar levels, and better understand type 2 diabetes.MonthlyEat WellGet recipes tailored to your unique tastes and interests every month!Get recipes tailored to your unique tastes and interests every month!WeeklyMenopauseOur newsletter will help you feel more prepared for this phase of life.Our newsletter will help you feel more prepared for this phase of life.WeeklySleep WellLearn how to sleep well every night so that you can wake up refreshed and rested.Learn how to sleep well every night so that you can wake up refreshed and rested.Twice WeeklyAnxiety & DepressionWe'll send expert guidance and science-backed tips to help you understand your triggers and improve your mental health.We'll send expert guidance and science-backed tips to help you understand your triggers and improve your mental health.WeeklyHeart HealthHow can you take the best care of your heart? This newsletter has everything to know.How can you take the best care of your heart? This newsletter has everything to know.WeeklyRheumatoid ArthritisLearn how you can manage pain, swelling, and stiffness with the latest treatment options.Learn how you can manage pain, swelling, and stiffness with the latest treatment options.WeeklyAllergies & AsthmaSneezing? Itchy eyes? Whether you live with allergies or asthma, we've got you covered on how to deal.Sneezing? Itchy eyes? Whether you live with allergies or asthma, we've got you covered on how to deal.WeeklyFibromyalgiaOur weekly newsletter features pain management techniques, tips to avoid fatigue, and more.Our weekly newsletter features pain management techniques, tips to avoid fatigue, and more.Twice WeeklyMultiple SclerosisGet treatment updates, self-care tips, and inspiring stories from real people living with this disease.Get treatment updates, self-care tips, and inspiring stories from real people living with this disease.Twice WeeklyPsoriasisWe'll prepare you to deal with flare-ups whenever they appear and share lifestyle changes that can help you feel better.We'll prepare you to deal with flare-ups whenever they appear and share lifestyle changes that can help you feel better.Twice WeeklyDigestive HealthWe talked to your gut \u00e2\u0080\u0094 it thinks you need this newsletter in your life.We talked to your gut \u00e2\u0080\u0094 it thinks you need this newsletter in your life.WeeklyCOPDLiving with COPD can be challenging. This newsletter is here to support you (and your lungs).Living with COPD can be challenging. This newsletter is here to support you (and your lungs).WeeklyCancer CareEverything you need to know about cancer care and treatments \u00e2\u0080\u0094 for yourself or a loved one.Everything you need to know about cancer care and treatments \u00e2\u0080\u0094 for yourself or a loved one.WeeklyBipolar DisorderGet science-backed advice and firsthand stories from people living with bipolar disorder.Get science-backed advice and firsthand stories from people living with bipolar disorder.WeeklyMigraineA migraine attack is more than \u00e2\u0080\u009cjust a headache.\u00e2\u0080\u009d Get tips from experts and people who understand migraine well.A migraine attack is more than \u00e2\u0080\u009cjust a headache.\u00e2\u0080\u009d Get tips from experts and people who understand migraine well.WeeklyOsteoarthritisKeep yourself healthy and moving safely with our expert-backed tips.Keep yourself healthy and moving safely with our expert-backed tips.WeeklyEndometriosisFrom pain management to fertility education, we're here to support you throughout your journey.From pain management to fertility education, we're here to support you throughout your journey.WeeklyBreast CancerLearn more about detection, diagnosis, treatment, and experiences from experts and real people.Learn more about detection, diagnosis, treatment, and experiences from experts and real people.WeeklyADHDCan't stay focused? Emotions running high? We're here to help.Can't stay focused? Emotions running high? We're here to help.WeeklyCaregiving for Alzheimer's DiseaseAlzheimer's can affect many parts of your life \u00e2\u0080\u0094 and your loved one\u00e2\u0080\u0099s. Let us help guide you along this journey.Alzheimer's can affect many parts of your life \u00e2\u0080\u0094 and your loved one\u00e2\u0080\u0099s. Let us help guide you along this journey.MonthlyParenthoodEvery month, we\u00e2\u0080\u0099ll share resources to help you navigate the many aspects of parenting.Every month, we\u00e2\u0080\u0099ll share resources to help you navigate the many aspects of parenting.WeeklyEye HealthGet tips and advice to keep your eyes healthy and functioning properly for a long time.Get tips and advice to keep your eyes healthy and functioning properly for a long time.WeeklyChronic Kidney DiseaseFind resources to help you manage chronic kidney disease so that you can live well.Find resources to help you manage chronic kidney disease so that you can live well.WeeklyWeight ManagementExpert advice, tips to build healthy habits, and the latest on nutrition and fitness delivered straight to your inbox.Expert advice, tips to build healthy habits, and the latest on nutrition and fitness delivered straight to your inbox.WeeklyHealthy AgingSign up for our newsletter and receive tips for preventive care, medicare coverage, and more.Sign up for our newsletter and receive tips for preventive care, medicare coverage, and more.WeeklyParkinson\u00e2\u0080\u0099sSign up for our newsletter and receive tools, support, and clarity for your journey with Parkinson\u00e2\u0080\u0099s Disease.Sign up for our newsletter and receive tools, support, and clarity for your journey with Parkinson\u00e2\u0080\u0099s Disease.WeeklyBack PainDiscover expert-backed tips to support your entire back.Discover expert-backed tips to support your entire back.WeeklyEczemaGet our best tips to keep your skin healthy all day, every day.Get our best tips to keep your skin healthy all day, every day.WeeklyProstate HealthFind out how to keep your prostate health in check.Find out how to keep your prostate health in check.WeeklyTraining ZoneSmart approaches to reach all your fitness goals.Smart approaches to reach all your fitness goals.2. Sign up hereEnter your emailSUBSCRIBEYour privacy is important to us2. Sign up hereEnter your emailSUBSCRIBEYour privacy is important to us", "content_hash": "b21674e8d88a0beaaa5357507cc4072a6609e355bc2a27b72bc0b5bebe4103ae", "domain": "healthline.com", "fetched_at": "2026-10-09T05:18:16.752711+00:00", "language": ""}, {"id": "1393", "title": "Article I", "url": "https://www.law.cornell.edu/constitution/articlei", "final_url": "https://www.law.cornell.edu/constitution/articlei", "text": "Section 1. All legislative powers herein granted shall be vested in a Congress of the United States, which shall consist of a Senate and House of Representatives. Section 2. The House of Representatives shall be composed of members chosen every second year by the people of the several states, and the electors in each state shall have the qualifications requisite for electors of the most numerous branch of the state legislature. No person shall be a Representative who shall not have attained to the age of twenty five years, and been seven years a citizen of the United States, and who shall not, when elected, be an inhabitant of that state in which he shall be chosen. Representatives and direct taxes shall be apportioned among the several states which may be included within this union, according to their respective numbers, which shall be determined by adding to the whole number of free persons, including those bound to service for a term of years, and excluding Indians not taxed, three fifths of all other Persons. The actual Enumeration shall be made within three years after the first meeting of the Congress of the United States, and within every subsequent term of ten years, in such manner as they shall by law direct. The number of Representatives shall not exceed one for every thirty thousand, but each state shall have at least one Representative; and until such enumeration shall be made, the state of New Hampshire shall be entitled to chuse three, Massachusetts eight, Rhode Island and Providence Plantations one, Connecticut five, New York six, New Jersey four, Pennsylvania eight, Delaware one, Maryland six, Virginia ten, North Carolina five, South Carolina five, and Georgia three. When vacancies happen in the Representation from any state, the executive authority thereof shall issue writs of election to fill such vacancies. The House of Representatives shall choose their speaker and other officers; and shall have the sole power of impeachment. Section 3. The Senate of the United States shall be composed of two Senators from each state, chosen by the legislature thereof, for six years; and each Senator shall have one vote. Immediately after they shall be assembled in consequence of the first election, they shall be divided as equally as may be into three classes. The seats of the Senators of the first class shall be vacated at the expiration of the second year, of the second class at the expiration of the fourth year, and the third class at the expiration of the sixth year, so that one third may be chosen every second year; and if vacancies happen by resignation, or otherwise, during the recess of the legislature of any state, the executive thereof may make temporary appointments until the next meeting of the legislature, which shall then fill such vacancies. No person shall be a Senator who shall not have attained to the age of thirty years, and been nine years a citizen of the United States and who shall not, when elected, be an inhabitant of that state for which he shall be chosen. The Vice President of the United States shall be President of the Senate, but shall have no vote, unless they be equally divided. The Senate shall choose their other officers, and also a President pro tempore, in the absence of the Vice President, or when he shall exercise the office of President of the United States. The Senate shall have the sole power to try all impeachments. When sitting for that purpose, they shall be on oath or affirmation. When the President of the United States is tried, the Chief Justice shall preside: And no person shall be convicted without the concurrence of two thirds of the members present. Judgment in cases of impeachment shall not extend further than to removal from office, and disqualification to hold and enjoy any office of honor, trust or profit under the United States: but the party convicted shall nevertheless be liable and subject to indictment, trial, judgment and punishment, according to law. Section 4. The times, places and manner of holding elections for Senators and Representatives, shall be prescribed in each state by the legislature thereof; but the Congress may at any time by law make or alter such regulations, except as to the places of choosing Senators. The Congress shall assemble at least once in every year, and such meeting shall be on the first Monday in December, unless they shall by law appoint a different day. Section 5. Each House shall be the judge of the elections, returns and qualifications of its own members, and a majority of each shall constitute a quorum to do business; but a smaller number may adjourn from day to day, and may be authorized to compel the attendance of absent members, in such manner, and under such penalties as each House may provide. Each House may determine the rules of its proceedings, punish its members for disorderly behavior, and, with the concurrence of two thirds, expel a member. Each House shall keep a journal of its proceedings, and from time to time publish the same, excepting such parts as may in their judgment require secrecy; and the yeas and nays of the members of either House on any question shall, at the desire of one fifth of those present, be entered on the journal. Neither House, during the session of Congress, shall, without the consent of the other, adjourn for more than three days, nor to any other place than that in which the two Houses shall be sitting. Section 6. The Senators and Representatives shall receive a compensation for their services, to be ascertained by law, and paid out of the treasury of the United States. They shall in all cases, except treason, felony and breach of the peace, be privileged from arrest during their attendance at the session of their respective Houses, and in going to and returning from the same; and for any speech or debate in either House, they shall not be questioned in any other place. No Senator or Representative shall, during the time for which he was elected, be appointed to any civil office under the authority of the United States, which shall have been created, or the emoluments whereof shall have been increased during such time: and no person holding any office under the United States, shall be a member of either House during his continuance in office. Section 7. All bills for raising revenue shall originate in the House of Representatives; but the Senate may propose or concur with amendments as on other Bills. Every bill which shall have passed the House of Representatives and the Senate, shall, before it become a law, be presented to the President of the United States; if he approve he shall sign it, but if not he shall return it, with his objections to that House in which it shall have originated, who shall enter the objections at large on their journal, and proceed to reconsider it. If after such reconsideration two thirds of that House shall agree to pass the bill, it shall be sent, together with the objections, to the other House, by which it shall likewise be reconsidered, and if approved by two thirds of that House, it shall become a law. But in all such cases the votes of both Houses shall be determined by yeas and nays, and the names of the persons voting for and against the bill shall be entered on the journal of each House respectively. If any bill shall not be returned by the President within ten days (Sundays excepted) after it shall have been presented to him, the same shall be a law, in like manner as if he had signed it, unless the Congress by their adjournment prevent its return, in which case it shall not be a law. Every order, resolution, or vote to which the concurrence of the Senate and House of Representatives may be necessary (except on a question of adjournment) shall be presented to the President of the United States; and before the same shall take effect, shall be approved by him, or being disapproved by him, shall be repassed by two thirds of the Senate and House of Representatives, according to the rules and limitations prescribed in the case of a bill. Section 8. The Congress shall have power to lay and collect taxes, duties, imposts and excises, to pay the debts and provide for the common defense and general welfare of the United States; but all duties, imposts and excises shall be uniform throughout the United States; To borrow money on the credit of the United States; To regulate commerce with foreign nations, and among the several states, and with the Indian tribes; To establish a uniform rule of naturalization, and uniform laws on the subject of bankruptcies throughout the United States; To coin money, regulate the value thereof, and of foreign coin, and fix the standard of weights and measures; To provide for the punishment of counterfeiting the securities and current coin of the United States; To establish post offices and post roads; To promote the progress of science and useful arts, by securing for limited times to authors and inventors the exclusive right to their respective writings and discoveries; To constitute tribunals inferior to the Supreme Court; To define and punish piracies and felonies committed on the high seas, and offenses against the law of nations; To declare war, grant letters of marque and reprisal, and make rules concerning captures on land and water; To raise and support armies, but no appropriation of money to that use shall be for a longer term than two years; To provide and maintain a navy; To make rules for the government and regulation of the land and naval forces; To provide for calling forth the militia to execute the laws of the union, suppress insurrections and repel invasions; To provide for organizing, arming, and disciplining, the militia, and for governing such part of them as may be employed in the service of the United States, reserving to the states respectively, the appointment of the officers, and the authority of training the militia according to the discipline prescribed by Congress; To exercise exclusive legislation in all cases whatsoever, over such District (not exceeding ten miles square) as may, by cession of particular states, and the acceptance of Congress, become the seat of the government of the United States, and to exercise like authority over all places purchased by the consent of the legislature of the state in which the same shall be, for the erection of forts, magazines, arsenals, dockyards, and other needful buildings;--And To make all laws which shall be necessary and proper for carrying into execution the foregoing powers, and all other powers vested by this Constitution in the government of the United States, or in any department or officer thereof. Section 9. The migration or importation of such persons as any of the states now existing shall think proper to admit, shall not be prohibited by the Congress prior to the year one thousand eight hundred and eight, but a tax or duty may be imposed on such importation, not exceeding ten dollars for each person. The privilege of the writ of habeas corpus shall not be suspended, unless when in cases of rebellion or invasion the public safety may require it. No bill of attainder or ex post facto Law shall be passed. No capitation, or other direct, tax shall be laid, unless in proportion to the census or enumeration herein before directed to be taken. No tax or duty shall be laid on articles exported from any state. No preference shall be given by any regulation of commerce or revenue to the ports of one state over those of another: nor shall vessels bound to, or from, one state, be obliged to enter, clear or pay duties in another. No money shall be drawn from the treasury, but in consequence of appropriations made by law; and a regular statement and account of receipts and expenditures of all public money shall be published from time to time. No title of nobility shall be granted by the United States: and no person holding any office of profit or trust under them, shall, without the consent of the Congress, accept of any present, emolument, office, or title, of any kind whatever, from any king, prince, or foreign state. Section 10. No state shall enter into any treaty, alliance, or confederation; grant letters of marque and reprisal; coin money; emit bills of credit; make anything but gold and silver coin a tender in payment of debts; pass any bill of attainder, ex post facto law, or law impairing the obligation of contracts, or grant any title of nobility. No state shall, without the consent of the Congress, lay any imposts or duties on imports or exports, except what may be absolutely necessary for executing its inspection laws: and the net produce of all duties and imposts, laid by any state on imports or exports, shall be for the use of the treasury of the United States; and all such laws shall be subject to the revision and control of the Congress. No state shall, without the consent of Congress, lay any duty of tonnage, keep troops, or ships of war in time of peace, enter into any agreement or compact with another state, or with a foreign power, or engage in war, unless actually invaded, or in such imminent danger as will not admit of delay. \u2039 Preamble up Article II \u203a", "content_hash": "78d8ba6533e9278900795c677326623a025b4eb4c4391b496bf082e1a79bc7ee", "domain": "law.cornell.edu", "fetched_at": "2026-10-09T05:18:17.315845+00:00", "language": ""}, {"id": "1394", "title": "NDIC Hosts International Deposit Insurers' Africa Regional Meeting in Abuja", "url": "https://ndic.gov.ng/news-detail?slug=ndic-hosts-international-deposit-insurers-africa-regional-meeting-in-abuja", "final_url": "https://ndic.gov.ng/news-detail?slug=ndic-hosts-international-deposit-insurers-africa-regional-meeting-in-abuja", "text": "NDIC Hosts International Deposit Insurers' Africa Regional Meeting in Abuja The Nigeria Deposit Insurance Corporation (NDIC) hosted the 2026 International Association of Deposit Insurers (IADI) Africa Regional Committee (ARC) Annual Meeting and Workshop in Abuja, bringing together prominent deposit insurance leaders from across the continent. N NDIC July 20, 2026 1 min read The Nigeria Deposit Insurance Corporation (NDIC) hosted the 2026 International Association of Deposit Insurers (IADI) Africa Regional Committee (ARC) Annual Meeting and Workshop in Abuja, bringing together prominent deposit insurance leaders from across the continent.The high-profile gathering commenced with sessions of the Research and Publicity Technical Committees, followed by the main ARC Annual Meeting presided over by Julia Oyet, Chairperson of IADI-ARC and CEO of the Deposit Protection Fund of Uganda.The event attracted distinguished participants, including IADI Secretary-General Eva Hupkes and Chief Executive Officers of deposit insurance agencies representing countries across Africa. This international assembly provided a crucial platform for sharing expertise, best practices, and innovative approaches to deposit insurance and financial system protection.The meeting facilitated discussions on emerging challenges in the deposit insurance sector, including technological advancements, regulatory frameworks, and depositor protection mechanisms. The participation of continental leaders underscored Africa's commitment to strengthening financial system resilience and stability.Hosting this significant international event demonstrated Nigeria's leadership position in the African financial services landscape and the NDIC's recognition as a key institutional player in deposit insurance matters. The workshop sessions enabled delegates to exchange insights on research initiatives, publicity strategies, and industry developments affecting deposit insurers across the continent.The successful hosting of the IADI-ARC meeting reinforced the NDIC's role as a responsible custodian of depositor funds and a committed partner in promoting financial system stability throughout Africa. All News Share More News October 2, 2026 NDIC Clinches Verbatim\u2019 s Pillar of Integrity in Public Service Award September 30, 2026 Keynote Address by the Managing Director/Chief Executive, Nigeria Deposit Insurance Corporation (NDIC), Mr. Thompson Oludare Sunday, on the Occasion of NDIC\u2019s Special Day at the 21st Abuja International Trade Fair, Wednesday, 30th September 2026 August 14, 2026 NDIC Reinforces Financial Safety Net Role Amid Banking Sector Reforms July 7, 2026 SERVICOM Commends NDIC on Efficient Service Deliver to Stakeholders June 30, 2026 NDIC Highlights Expanded Deposit Protection and Accelerated Reimbursement Systems Browse all news", "content_hash": "fc8e1cdd498a2967d0b79c0c5247c28120fcea9e4338e7816849f22c497bcdf8", "domain": "ndic.gov.ng", "fetched_at": "2026-10-09T05:18:18.008484+00:00", "language": ""}, {"id": "1395", "title": "Healthline Videos", "url": "https://www.healthline.com/video", "final_url": "https://www.healthline.com/video", "text": "Healthline VideosNutrition5 High Fiber Foods You Should EatFiber is an important nutrient with many health benefits. Watch this video to learn about\u00e2\u0080\u00a6The 5 Best Foods for High Blood PressureHypertension, or high blood pressure, is the mos