e Best Heart-Healthy Sleeping PositionsThere\u00e2\u0080\u0099s little research on the effects of sleeping positions on heart health. There\u00e2\u0080\u0099s some\u00e2\u0080\u00a6Fit it In: 22-Minute Yoga for SleepEnjoy a 22-minute yoga flow that will help your body get ready for a good night's sleep. Cracking The Sleep Code: Can Going to Bed Later Help you Fall Asleep Faster?David's erratic work schedule and lack of sleep hygiene have him laying in bed for hours\u00e2\u0080\u00a6Cracking The Sleep Code: Can Meditating Before Bed Reduce Insomnia?In this episode of Cracking the Sleep Code, we uncover whether meditation can actually\u00e2\u0080\u00a6Science of Dreams: Do Dreams Boost Creativity?How can our dream world inform our real world? In this episode, bestselling author Dr\u00e2\u0080\u00a6Science of Dreams: What is Sleep Paralysis?What happens when the mind wakes up but the body is still asleep? Sleep paralysis is a\u00e2\u0080\u00a6Women\u00e2\u0080\u0099s WellnessWhy Is My Period Late?Missed or late periods happen for many reasons other than pregnancy. Watch this video to learn the common causes of a late\u00e2\u0080\u00a64 Yoga Poses to Relieve CrampsYoga may provide relief for period cramps. In this video, learn several yoga poses that can help with bloating and cramping\u00e2\u0080\u00a6Healthline On Call: Using a Menstrual Cup with FibroidsDr. Nwadike answers the question, \"Can I use a menstrual cup if I have uterine fibroids?\"PMS Symptoms vs. Pregnancy SymptomsPremenstrual syndrome (PMS) is a group of symptoms that tend to occur 1 to 2 weeks before your period. How to Make Your Period End FasterThere are ways to reduce the number of days in your menstrual cycle and end your period faster.Implantation Bleeding vs. Period Bleeding: How to Tell the DifferenceImplantation bleeding (IB) occurs when a fertilized egg nestles into the uterine lining. But how can you tell if you\u00e2\u0080\u0099re\u00e2\u0080\u00a6Is It Safe to Have Sex During Your Period? There\u00e2\u0080\u0099s no need to avoid sex during your period. While period sex can be messy, it\u00e2\u0080\u0099s safe and even has some benefits. Watch\u00e2\u0080\u00a6How Late Can a Period Be? Plus, Why It\u00e2\u0080\u0099s LateYour period should start within 35 to 38 days of your last period, depending on your normal cycle. Everything You Need to Know About Vaginal DischargeVaginal discharge is usually normal but there are certain types of discharge that can indicate an infection or other health\u00e2\u0080\u00a610 Reasons Why You Might Miss a PeriodIs it normal to miss periods? This video gives you 10 reasons why you might have missed your period.Parenthood10 Foods and Beverages to Avoid During Pregnancy: What Not to EatThis video covers the best and worst foods to eat during pregnancy. Parenthood How-To: Bathing BabyBath time can be intimidating, but you can do this. Watch now for a step-by-step overview of giving baby a bath. (Even if\u00e2\u0080\u00a6Parenthood How-To: Burping BabyWhile there is no \"right way,\" there are some proven ways to burp a baby.5 Natural Ways to Induce LaborIf you\u00e2\u0080\u0099re 40 weeks into your pregnancy, here are some natural ways to encourage labor.Parenthood How-To: DIY PadsicleThe padsicle is a home remedy new moms shouldn't live without. Watch now for a DIY padsicle you can (pre) make at home.Parenthood How-To: SwaddleWatch this video to learn the simple way to swaddle your infant.Parenthood How-To: Changing A DiaperIt doesn't always have to be a dirty job! Watch now for a step-by-step diaper changing demo.SkincareWhat Is Cystic Acne and How is it Treated?This video explains cystic acne causes and treatments. How to Remove Dark Spots From PimplesCurious on how to remove dark spots? This video breaks down how to remove dark spots from\u00e2\u0080\u00a6Pimple in Ear: How It Happens and How to Treat ItThis video breaks down the causes and treatments of pimples in your ear. Best Face Washes for AcneLooking for a good face wash for acne-prone skin? This video gives you suggestions on the\u00e2\u0080\u00a6Ways to Remove Blackheads From the NoseThis video shows you easy ways to get rid of blackheads from your nose. Causes and Treatments of Forehead AcneThis video gives you the causes of forehead acne and common ways to treat it. How To Get Rid Of A Winter RashThis video explains how to get rid of a winter rash.Different Types of Eczema, Causes, and SymptomsLearn the different types of eczema, as well as causes and symptoms.Top 5 Benefits of Taking Collagen SupplementsCollagen is the most abundant protein in your body. It has many vital functions, such as\u00e2\u0080\u00a6Food Fix: Healthier SkinIn this video, learn the best foods to eat for healthy and glowing skin from a registered\u00e2\u0080\u00a6", "content_hash": "40c18e5baeb7542bba9b957f45240e4282e30a931762189480a0779c34fb2068", "domain": "healthline.com", "fetched_at": "2026-10-09T05:18:20.344594+00:00", "language": ""}, {"id": "1396", "title": "NDIC Hosts IADI Africa Regional Committee Meeting 2026", "url": "https://ndic.gov.ng/article?slug=ndic-hosts-iadi-africa-regional-committee-meeting-2026", "final_url": "https://ndic.gov.ng/article?slug=ndic-hosts-iadi-africa-regional-committee-meeting-2026", "text": "NDIC Hosts IADI Africa Regional Committee Meeting 2026 N NDIC July 17, 2026 1 min read The Nigeria Deposit Insurance Corporation (NDIC) will host the International Association of Deposit Insurers (IADI) Africa Regional Committee (ARC) Annual Meeting and Workshop from 20th to 23rd of July, 2026 in Abuja \u2013 Nigeria with the Theme: \u201cSafeguarding Stability: Public Awareness and Crisis Readiness for a Stronger Future\u201d. The 4-day high-level regional engagement will gather Chief Executives, Directors and senior officials of deposit insurance institutions, financial regulators and other stakeholders within the financial safety net across Africa and beyond. The workshop will deliberate on strategies for strengthening public awareness and crisis preparedness in deposit insurance systems in line with the revised IADI Core Principles. Focus will be on building and sustaining depositors confidence, enhancing communication, improving coordination among financial safety-net participants, and equipping deposit insurers with practical tools for crisis management and resolution. The workshop will also provide a platform for peer learning, sharing of experience, and addressing emerging risks including financial technology innovations and cyber threats, with a view to strengthening frameworks the for effective crisis response. The programme will also feature technical committee meetings, plenary sessions and interactive roundtable discussions covering public awareness in both normal and crisis periods, crisis preparedness and management, system-wide response operations, and simulation exercises designed to test institutional readiness. The event will be declared open by Mr. Olayemi Cardoso, Governor, Central Bank of Nigeria (CBN). The Honourable Minister of Finance and Coordinating Minister of the Economy, Mr. Taiwo Oyedele is expected to deliver the Keynote Address. Other dignitaries expected include Ms. Eva H\u00fcpkes, IADI Secretary General, and Ms. Julia Oyet, Chairperson of the IADI-Africa Regional Committee. The Chief Host of the Conference is Mr. Thompson Oludare Sunday, Managing Director/Chief Executive, NDIC. Hawwau GamboHead, Communication & Public Affairs Department All Articles Share More Articles July 22, 2026 Hon. Minister of Finance's Keynote Speech at IADI-ARC Workshop 2026 July 21, 2026 MD/CE NDIC\u2019S Welcome Remark at IADI-ARC Workshop & Annual Meeting 2026 July 1, 2026 Update on the Revocation of the Operating Licenses of 46 Micro-Finance Banks by the Central Bank of Nigeria June 19, 2026 Disposal of Landed Properties and Other Physical Assets, Defunct Primary Mortgage Banks June 15, 2026 Sale of Chattels of Closed Primary Mortgage Banks by Public Auction and Public Competitive Bid. Browse all articles", "content_hash": "9bafc7e7d81fbd413311b2263643ebe843cf36e8996b616de50b919cdbc5b6c7", "domain": "ndic.gov.ng", "fetched_at": "2026-10-09T05:18:21.433310+00:00", "language": ""}, {"id": "1397", "title": "SERVICOM Commends NDIC on Efficient Service Deliver to Stakeholders", "url": "https://ndic.gov.ng/news-detail?slug=servicom-commends-ndic-on-efficient-service-deliver-to-stakeholders2", "final_url": "https://ndic.gov.ng/news-detail?slug=servicom-commends-ndic-on-efficient-service-deliver-to-stakeholders2", "text": "SERVICOM Commends NDIC on Efficient Service Deliver to Stakeholders The MD/CE, NDIC, Mr. Thomson Oludare Sunday, today received the Ag. National Coordinator/CEO of Service Compact with All Nigerians (SERVICOM), Mrs. Helen Lawal and members of her Management Team on a courtesy visit. N NDIC July 7, 2026 1 min read The MD/CE, NDIC, Mr. Thomson Oludare Sunday, today received the Ag. National Coordinator/CEO of Service Compact with All Nigerians (SERVICOM), Mrs. Helen Lawal and members of her Management Team on a courtesy visit.During the visit, Mrs. Lawal, emphasised quality service delivery as a critical measure of good governance and underscored SERVICOM\u2019s mandate to promote excellence in service delivery across all public institutions. She highlighted NDIC\u2019s statutory responsibilities and commended the Corporation\u2019s customer-focused initiatives, including the Service Level Agreement Management System (SLAMS), customer call centre and effective complaint management mechanisms.The NDIC Boss, lauded SERVICOM\u2019s continued partnership in promoting service excellence. He reaffirmed NDIC\u2019s commitment to delivering its statutory mandate efficiently and strengthening public confidence in the financial system through prompt and customer-focused service delivery.Both NDIC and SERVICOM resolved that the Corporation would maintain its high performance standards, sustain full coverage of over 98 percent of accounts in the banking system, efficient claims payment, staff capacity development, and service delivery enhancements as well as deepen collaboration with SERVICOM.In recognition of NDIC\u2019s compliance with SERVICOM standards and milestones, the agency awarded the Corporation with the Best Performing Parastatal Unit (Team D) while the NDIC Boss was recognized as the Most Supportive CEO and decorated as SERVICOM Champion. All News Share More News October 2, 2026 NDIC Clinches Verbatim\u2019 s Pillar of Integrity in Public Service Award September 30, 2026 Keynote Address by the Managing Director/Chief Executive, Nigeria Deposit Insurance Corporation (NDIC), Mr. Thompson Oludare Sunday, on the Occasion of NDIC\u2019s Special Day at the 21st Abuja International Trade Fair, Wednesday, 30th September 2026 August 14, 2026 NDIC Reinforces Financial Safety Net Role Amid Banking Sector Reforms July 20, 2026 NDIC Hosts International Deposit Insurers' Africa Regional Meeting in Abuja June 30, 2026 NDIC Highlights Expanded Deposit Protection and Accelerated Reimbursement Systems Browse all news", "content_hash": "f2e732b44a8d51f1328a06583912db1d34d613c39813a95cc06fc82c3597d16d", "domain": "ndic.gov.ng", "fetched_at": "2026-10-09T05:18:24.595744+00:00", "language": ""}, {"id": "1398", "title": "Update on the Revocation of the Operating Licenses of 46 Micro-Finance Banks by the Central Bank of Nigeria", "url": "https://ndic.gov.ng/article?slug=update-on-the-revocation-of-the-operating-licenses-of-46-microfinance-banks-by-the-central-bank-of-nigeria", "final_url": "https://ndic.gov.ng/article?slug=update-on-the-revocation-of-the-operating-licenses-of-46-microfinance-banks-by-the-central-bank-of-nigeria", "text": "Update on the Revocation of the Operating Licenses of 46 Micro-Finance Banks by the Central Bank of Nigeria N NDIC July 1, 2026 2 min read Further to our statement of July 1, 2026 on the revocation of the operating licences of 46 Microfinance Banks (MFBs) by the Central Bank of Nigeria (CBN) on July 1, 2026, the Nigeria Deposit Insurance Corporation (NDIC), wishes to inform the depositors of the defunct banks in particular, and the general public that, NDIC staff have commenced depositors\u2019 verification at the offices of the affected banks across the country. Consequently:Depositors who had their Bank Verification Numbers (BVNs) linked to their accounts in the failed banks will be paid through their alternative accounts in existing banks;Depositors whose BVNs were not linked to their accounts in the failed banks are encouraged to visit the affected banks offices with proof of account ownership, a passport photograph, verifiable means of identification (Driver\u2019s Licence, Permanent Voter\u2019s Card, International Passport or National ID Card) and Bank Verification Number (BVN).Depositors can also file their claims online, by visiting the NDIC Website: www.ndic.gov.ng, to complete the Pre-Verification Claims Form by clicking on the Search Bar, and typing: \u201cPre-Verification Claims Form\u201d; opening the Form and filling in their details. Depositors can also do so by clicking the link: https://ndic.gov.ng/ndic-pre-verification-claims-form/Depositors can also visit any of the NDIC offices closest to them to file their claims.For further enquiries, the Corporation can be reached on any of the following lines: 09037273810 09038197064;08104220807;09064657140.The list of the affected MFBs is as follows:ABIAMerchant Microfinance Bank, AbaAbia SME Microfinance Bank, AbaAKWA-IBOMCasha Microfinance Bank, KubwaStanford Microfinance Bank, UyoANAMBRAFrontline Microfinance Bank, Nnewi South LGABAYELSACrystabel Microfinance Bank, YenogoaBENUEStrait Sahara Microfinance Bank Limited, MakurdiCROSS RIVERLivingspring Microfinance Bank, CalabarDELTACreekline Microfinance Bank, WarriFCTWinview Microfinance Bank Limited, KubwaKADUNAZafec Microfinance Bank Limited, ZariaBasawa Microfinance Bank Limited, ZariaKANOZain Microfinance Bank, Dawakin Tofa LGABompai Microfinance Bank, Nassarawa LGAAjwa Microfinance Bank, GezawaNOW NOW Digital Microfinance Bank Limited, DoguwaMinjibir Microfinance BankShanono Microfinance BankSumaila Microfinance BankRimin Gado Microfinance BankSycamore Microfinance Bank Limited, AlbasuTofa Microfinance BankKanopoly Microfinance BankBellbank Microfinance Bank, TsanyawaEsteem Microfinance Bank LimitedKEBBIKamba Microfinance Bank, Dandi LGAZuru Microfinance BankKWARAJanmaa Microfinance Bank, IlorinLAGOSGold Microfinance Bank, IkejaChanelle Microfinance Bank, IkejaSafegate Microfinance Bank, EgbedaSupreme Microfinance Bank, OguduCreditville Microfinance Bank Limited, Victoria IslandMBAG Microfinance Bank Limited, OgbaVerdant-Capital Microfinance Bank Limited, MarylandEntrepreneur Microfinance Bank Limited, Lagos-IslandNIGERBusu Microfinance BankBejin-Doko Microfinance Bank Limited, DokoOGUNIwade Microfinance Bank, Ijebu OdeApple Microfinance Bank Limited, IjebuONDOOurPass Microfinance Bank Limited, IfonOSUNAvantus Microfinance Bank Limited, IjesaOYOBest Star Microfinance Bank LimitedPLATEAUMwaghavul Microfinance Bank, ManguYeneng Microfinance BankRIVERSMinji-Se Churchill Microfinance Bank, Port-Harcourt All Articles Share More Articles July 22, 2026 Hon. Minister of Finance's Keynote Speech at IADI-ARC Workshop 2026 July 21, 2026 MD/CE NDIC\u2019S Welcome Remark at IADI-ARC Workshop & Annual Meeting 2026 July 17, 2026 NDIC Hosts IADI Africa Regional Committee Meeting 2026 June 19, 2026 Disposal of Landed Properties and Other Physical Assets, Defunct Primary Mortgage Banks June 15, 2026 Sale of Chattels of Closed Primary Mortgage Banks by Public Auction and Public Competitive Bid. Browse all articles", "content_hash": "2845c99a5daf2952be200874f440702d7cc5dff280ef9df00542a17ad8cda04e", "domain": "ndic.gov.ng", "fetched_at": "2026-10-09T05:18:27.762890+00:00", "language": ""}, {"id": "1399", "title": "NDIC Highlights Expanded Deposit Protection and Accelerated Reimbursement Systems", "url": "https://ndic.gov.ng/news-detail?slug=ndic-highlights-expanded-deposit-protection-and-accelerated-reimbursement-systems", "final_url": "https://ndic.gov.ng/news-detail?slug=ndic-highlights-expanded-deposit-protection-and-accelerated-reimbursement-systems", "text": "NDIC Highlights Expanded Deposit Protection and Accelerated Reimbursement Systems The Nigeria Deposit Insurance Corporation (NDIC) showcased its performance achievements at the Second Quarter Citizens' and Stakeholders' Engagement Session held at the Federal Ministry of Finance in Abuja. N NDIC June 30, 2026 1 min read The Nigeria Deposit Insurance Corporation (NDIC) showcased its performance achievements at the Second Quarter Citizens' and Stakeholders' Engagement Session held at the Federal Ministry of Finance in Abuja. Managing Director/Chief Executive Mr. Thompson Oludare Sunday presented the Corporation's comprehensive scorecard to representatives from the media, civil society organizations, and key stakeholders.Mr. Sunday highlighted the 2024 upward review of deposit insurance coverage as a landmark achievement. Coverage limits increased to \u20a65 million for Deposit Money Banks (DMBs) and \u20a62 million for Microfinance and Primary Mortgage Banks (MFBs/PMBs). The revised coverage now fully protects over 98 percent of DMB accounts and more than 99 percent of MFB/PMB accounts nationwide.The deployment of technology-driven payment systems represents another significant advancement. These systems have substantially accelerated the reimbursement of depositors of failed banks, significantly strengthening public confidence in the banking system and demonstrating the NDIC's operational efficiency.Additionally, Mr. Sunday highlighted the NDIC's increased remittances to the Consolidated Revenue Fund (CRF), underscoring the Corporation's prudence and accountability culture.The Permanent Secretary of the Federal Ministry of Finance, Mr. Raymond Omachi, commended the quarterly engagement session as a reflection of the Ministry's commitment to transparency, accountability, and sustained dialogue with citizens and stakeholders regarding implementation of Federal Government priority programmes and ministerial deliverables.This transparent engagement reinforces stakeholder confidence in both the NDIC and the broader financial system governance framework. All News Share More News October 2, 2026 NDIC Clinches Verbatim\u2019 s Pillar of Integrity in Public Service Award September 30, 2026 Keynote Address by the Managing Director/Chief Executive, Nigeria Deposit Insurance Corporation (NDIC), Mr. Thompson Oludare Sunday, on the Occasion of NDIC\u2019s Special Day at the 21st Abuja International Trade Fair, Wednesday, 30th September 2026 August 14, 2026 NDIC Reinforces Financial Safety Net Role Amid Banking Sector Reforms July 20, 2026 NDIC Hosts International Deposit Insurers' Africa Regional Meeting in Abuja July 7, 2026 SERVICOM Commends NDIC on Efficient Service Deliver to Stakeholders Browse all news", "content_hash": "215aae4213614ac7db8fed322a7da2b9138a11c104c052146ede2debc9e65596", "domain": "ndic.gov.ng", "fetched_at": "2026-10-09T05:18:30.561311+00:00", "language": ""}, {"id": "1400", "title": "What Is a Sleep Journal, and Why Do You Need One?", "url": "https://healthline.com/health/sleep/sleep-journal", "final_url": "https://healthline.com/health/sleep/sleep-journal", "text": "Key takeaways A sleep journal is a written or digital record of your sleeping bedtimes, sleep quality, and factors that can affect them, such as caffeine, alcohol, exercise, and medications. You can use any format that\u00e2\u0080\u0099s easy to keep up with, from a simple notebook to an app. Tracking sleep data before bed and after waking up can improve the accuracy of your sleep journal and provide valuable insights into your sleep patterns. This article is Day 4 of Healthline\u00e2\u0080\u0099s 3 Week Better Sleep Challenge. Follow along to discover small, meaningful habits to help you sleep better, feel more rested, and wake ready to thrive each day.Missed a day? Catch up with Day 3: How to fix Your Sleep Schedule, or continue on to Day 5: What to Know About Healthy Sleep.Tracking your habits can be great for almost any health area, especially sleep. A sleep journal allows you to log bedtimes, duration, and quality of sleep, and how factors like caffeine- and alcohol-containing drinks, medications, and exercise affect sleep.What is a sleep journal? Though you can purchase entire book-bound diaries created for tracking sleep, a sleep journal doesn\u00e2\u0080\u0099t have to be fancy (or even particularly long). It can be any digital or physical record of your sleep and the influences affecting it.Some people may prefer to write a sleep journal as a narrative, writing sentences or paragraphs describing each day\u00e2\u0080\u0099s sleep patterns. Others may like the convenience of a graph or chart with simple boxes to check and numbers to fill in.For example, the National Heart, Lung, and Blood Institute (NHLBI) has a free downloadable sleep journal template with spaces for the day\u00e2\u0080\u0099s date, number of caffeine\u00e2\u0080\u0093 and alcohol-containing beverages, exercise times and lengths, naps, and a sleepiness rating.Other useful diaries are available from the National Sleep Foundation (NSF) and the American Academy of Sleep Medicine.Consider using a sleep-tracking app and a wearable device such as Oura, Calm, or SleepWatch to simplify things even further. A small 2023 study found that sleep-tracking apps helped people maintain consistent usage.How do you use a sleep journal? Using a sleep journal for at least a week can help you identify patterns affecting your rest. The data it provides can help you and your doctor understand sleep problems. In fact, a 2022 study found that sleep tracking was a meaningful way to assess insomnia from the participants\u00e2\u0080\u0099 perspectives and resulted in useful information.Sleep journal templates may include many factors that influence sleep. In general, though, some elements to track include:total sleep hours per 24-hour periodsleepiness level throughout the daydaily medicationsnumber of caffeine- and alcohol-containing drinks and time consumedbedtimewake timeexercise type and durationsleep quality levelnapstime it takes to fall asleepnight awakeningsIf you feel other factors (such as stress or caring for a young child) significantly affect your sleep, include them.When do you need to use a sleep journal?Some sleep journals provide instructions on logging your information. For example, the NHLBI journal has spaces to fill in at night and in the morning.If you\u00e2\u0080\u0099re without pre-set directions, though, it\u00e2\u0080\u0099s probably great to track your sleep data right before bed and shortly after waking in the morning. A small 2024 study found that people had difficulty recalling sleep details when they didn\u00e2\u0080\u0099t complete their journal entries first thing in the morning and just before turning in at night.Keeping a sleep journal can help you if you have difficulty falling or staying asleep. If When to see a doctor if you have trouble sleepingThe NSF mentions that chronic insomnia involves having trouble sleeping at least 3 nights per week for at least 3 months. But you don\u00e2\u0080\u0099t have to reach this point before getting help from a healthcare professional. Any time sleep problems interfere with your daily activities, it\u00e2\u0080\u0099s a good idea to get medical attention.What\u00e2\u0080\u0099s the outlook for people with trouble sleeping?Sleep problems can interfere with your quality of life, and some, like sleep apnea, may link with chronic medical conditions. Contacting your doctor about sleep issues (and having your filled-in sleep journal handy when you do) can help get your nighttime rest back on a healthy, refreshing track.Frequently asked questionsWhat does a sleep journal do?A sleep journal can provide insights into the factors strongly influencing your sleep. When you use one for a week or longer, the experience can highlight patterns or connections you might not usually notice.What do you write in a sleep journal?You can log the time you go to bed and your quality of sleep. It might be useful to mention any other factors that can influence the quality of your sleep, like drinking caffeine and alcohol, or any medications you\u00e2\u0080\u0099re taking.Why do I need 9 hours of sleep to feel rested?The NSF considers getting 7 to 9 hours of sleep a typical adult sleep duration. So, although 9 hours is on the longer side, it\u00e2\u0080\u0099s still a healthy amount of sleep.Is 12 hours of sleep too much?Sleeping 12 hours per night falls above the NSF\u00e2\u0080\u0099s recommendations for adults. However, some world-class athletes sleep that much. Talk with your healthcare professional if you sleep this much regularly and remain sleepy during the day.TakeawayPeople have their own unique underlying causes of sleep troubles. Using a sleep journal for a week or two (or even longer) can help you realize what\u00e2\u0080\u0099s making you toss and turn. You can find solutions when you can identify the source of your sleep problems.", "content_hash": "feff2366b19df3f07509b53d6d15baae1492433d9e356cd660bb02f6137b8d10", "domain": "healthline.com", "fetched_at": "2026-10-09T05:18:32.300267+00:00", "language": ""}, {"id": "1401", "title": "NDIC and Federal High Court Strengthen Partnership for Efficient Justice and Financial System Protection", "url": "https://ndic.gov.ng/news-detail?slug=ndic-and-federal-high-court-strengthen-partnership-for-efficient-justice-and-financial-system-protection", "final_url": "https://ndic.gov.ng/news-detail?slug=ndic-and-federal-high-court-strengthen-partnership-for-efficient-justice-and-financial-system-protection", "text": "NDIC and Federal High Court Strengthen Partnership for Efficient Justice and Financial System Protection The NDIC Management, led by Managing Director/Chief Executive Mr. Thompson Oludare Sunday, hosted the Chief Registrar of the Federal High Court, Mr. Yahaya Yakubu Shafa, and his team on a strategic courtesy visit aimed at deepening institutional collaboration. N NDIC June 22, 2026 1 min read The NDIC Management, led by Managing Director/Chief Executive Mr. Thompson Oludare Sunday, hosted the Chief Registrar of the Federal High Court, Mr. Yahaya Yakubu Shafa, and his team on a strategic courtesy visit aimed at deepening institutional collaboration.The discussions centered on accelerating the objective resolution of NDIC-related cases, with particular focus on failed banks, debt recovery, and liquidation proceedings. Both institutions recognized the critical importance of judicial efficiency in protecting depositor interests and ensuring swift asset recovery.The partnership reflects the complementary roles of both institutions in maintaining financial system stability. While the Federal High Court provides judicial oversight and resolution mechanisms, the NDIC safeguards depositor funds and manages the resolution process for failed financial institutions.Both institutions pledged their commitment to enhancing judicial efficiency to ensure depositors are protected and assets of banks in-liquidation are recovered promptly. This collaborative approach acknowledges that effective justice delivery directly impacts the confidence stakeholders maintain in the financial system.The visit underscored the importance of inter-institutional coordination in addressing the complex legal and financial challenges associated with bank failures. By streamlining case resolution processes and improving communication channels, both organizations aim to reduce the time required to resolve disputes and recover assets.This strengthened partnership demonstrates the NDIC's proactive engagement in creating an enabling environment for efficient financial system management, ultimately benefiting depositors and contributing to overall financial system resilience in Nigeria. All News Share More News October 2, 2026 NDIC Clinches Verbatim\u2019 s Pillar of Integrity in Public Service Award September 30, 2026 Keynote Address by the Managing Director/Chief Executive, Nigeria Deposit Insurance Corporation (NDIC), Mr. Thompson Oludare Sunday, on the Occasion of NDIC\u2019s Special Day at the 21st Abuja International Trade Fair, Wednesday, 30th September 2026 August 14, 2026 NDIC Reinforces Financial Safety Net Role Amid Banking Sector Reforms July 20, 2026 NDIC Hosts International Deposit Insurers' Africa Regional Meeting in Abuja July 7, 2026 SERVICOM Commends NDIC on Efficient Service Deliver to Stakeholders Browse all news", "content_hash": "c57981315dceb40de16f101e5a4de4ddacbb1f4c2a4f794a8e5bc1f70b325c2e", "domain": "ndic.gov.ng", "fetched_at": "2026-10-09T05:18:33.174536+00:00", "language": ""}, {"id": "1402", "title": "2026 Healthline GLP-1 Survey: Unique Insights into Expectations vs. Real-World Experiences", "url": "https://healthline.com/health/drugs/2026-healthline-glp-1-survey-findings", "final_url": "https://healthline.com/health/drugs/2026-healthline-glp-1-survey-findings", "text": "Stories about glucagon-like peptide-1 (GLP-1) medications can make treatment sound life changing, daunting, or both. The 2026 Healthline GLP-1 Consumer Survey explored how closely pretreatment expectations align with actual experiences, from side effects and benefits to how long people plan to stay on treatment.We surveyed 1,007 people who are considering a GLP-1 medication, currently take one, or have taken one in the past. Their responses provide a closer look at where expectations and real-world experiences align and where they differ. Here are the main findings.2026 Healthline GLP-1 Consumer Survey Methodology and DemographicsTotal sample: 1,007 peopleGender:53% female47% maleAge by generation:15% Gen Z38% Millennials30% Gen X17% BoomersTreatment status:720 people, or 71%, were currently taking a GLP-1203 people, or 20%, had taken a GLP-1 in the past84 people, or 8%, were considering a GLP-1Primary medical reason:59% cited weight loss or obesity management19% cited diabetes or blood sugar management20% cited both weight and blood sugar management1% cited another reasonSurvey type: Online consumer-panel surveyField dates: February 25-26, 2026Representativeness: The findings describe the people surveyed and should not be treated as estimates for everyone considering or taking a GLP-1 drug.Common GLP-1 side effects may occur less frequently than expectedGastrointestinal side effects are often a concern when considering GLP-1 medications. The Healthline Survey, however, suggests that what people expect to experience may not always match what those taking a GLP-1 actually report.For example, nausea and vomiting were the most expected side effects, with 67% of people considering a GLP-1 anticipating them. By comparison, 34% of those currently or previously taking a GLP-1 actually reported these symptoms.The rate still represents about 1 in 3 people experiencing these effects, suggesting that nausea and vomiting may be symptoms worth preparing for when starting treatment.Other well-known GLP-1 side effects showed a similar gap between the expectations of people considering treatment and the experiences of people who have taken a GLP-1:Injection site reactions: anticipated by 42% of people considering a GLP-1, but reported by 15% of those currently or previously taking a GLP-1Diarrhea: expected by 39%, but reported by 25%Dizziness or lightheadedness: expected by 35%, compared with 20% who experienced itRapid heart rate or palpitations: expected by 23%, but reported by 9%\u00c2\u00bbRead more:What to Expect on a GLP-1: Personal StoriesLess-discussed GLP-1 side effects may more closely match real-life reportsFor some less-talked-about symptoms, expectations appear to be closer to what people actually experience:Constipation: anticipated by 24% of people considering a GLP-1 and reported by 25% of those who had taken oneSleep changes (insomnia and vivid dreams): expected by 11% and reported by 13%Feeling cold: expected by 10% and reported by 12%The biggest difference involved sulfur burps or gas. Only 10% of people expected this GLP-1 side effect, but 17% of respondents actually experienced it during their treatment.Keep in mind: The 2026 Healthline GLP-1 Consumer Survey asked people considering these medications what they expected from treatment. It also asked people currently or formerly taking one what they had experienced during treatment. These are comparisons between different groups, so they are not meant to predict what will happen to any one person or show how someone\u00e2\u0080\u0099s expectations change after starting treatment.Why this mattersFear of side effects can shape decisions before a treatment even begins. People considering a GLP-1 may assume nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, or injection site reactions are almost inevitable parts of treatment. Concerns about side effects may keep someone from starting a medication they need, lead them to experience every new sensation as a sign that their body isn\u00e2\u0080\u0099t tolerating it, or cause them to overlook less-talked-about side effects.The Healthline Survey results cannot predict what any one person will or will not experience on a GLP-1. But they may offer a more realistic starting point to replace a sense of inevitability with a practical plan: know what symptoms are possibleunderstand which can usually be managedclarify what would warrant a medication reviewBeing aware of both common and less common symptoms can help you begin treatment informed and prepared, while leaving room for your experience to be different from what you may have expected.\u00c2\u00bbLearn: What to Expect After a Month on GLP-1sDramatic changes in the face, body, and skin less common than expectedFacial volume loss, often referred to as \u00e2\u0080\u009cOzempic face,\u00e2\u0080\u009d has become one of the more widely discussed GLP-1-related concerns. The Healthline Survey, however, suggests that expectations of these effects may exceed what people who take GLP-1s experience.Among respondents planning to take a GLP-1, 44% expected to experience drastic facial changes as a result of treatment. In comparison, 20% of those who had taken a GLP-1 actually experienced this side effect.A similar gap emerged around changes in skin texture. While 39% of prospective patients expected loose or sagging skin after taking a GLP-1, 18% of those who had taken one reported these changes.The closer match between expectations and experience was around muscle loss or reduced physical strength, a longer-term side effect of rapid weight loss with GLP-1s. Of people considering a GLP-1, 23% anticipated losing muscle mass as a result, while 21% of those taking a GLP-1 actually experienced this side effect.What do you wish you knew before taking GLP-1s?\u00e2\u0080\u009cThe fact that you need to exercise as the weight comes off fast and weakens your muscles.\u00e2\u0080\u009d\u00e2\u0080\u0094 Respondent from the 2026 Healthline GLP-1 Survey, currently taking a GLP-1 (3-6 months)Read more: A Guide for Deciding If GLP-1s Are Right for YouWhy this mattersFor someone deciding whether to start, continue, or stop treatment with a GLP-1, it can be difficult to tell which experiences are common and which receive the most attention because they sound drastic or extreme.Changes in the face or skin, for example, are easy to photograph and share. Celebrity stories about appearance changes after GLP-1 treatment may also attract significant attention, often without much context. Changes in strength may be less visible, but they can affect everyday function and quality of life.It is important to keep the Healthline Survey\u00e2\u0080\u0099s findings in context. People report what they noticed about themselves; the survey did not measure facial volume, skin texture, or muscle mass. The findings also cannot determine whether GLP-1 medication directly caused any particular change.Experiences may also vary based on factors like: starting body weightpercentage and pace of weight lossdietary habitsageactivity levelsother health factorsThese may influence how people experience treatment and how they report those experiences with GLP-1s.For people taking a GLP-1, paying attention to both function and appearance can provide a fuller picture of how treatment is affecting them. Signs of declining strength may include having more difficulty: climbing stairscarrying groceriesexercisinggetting up from a chair\u00c2\u00bbRead more:Can GLP-1s Cause Long-Term Side Effects?GLP-1 weight loss close to expectations, well-being less predictableWeight loss was the most widely expected and reported benefit of GLP-1s. Among people considering treatment, 81% expected to lose weight, while 75% of all people who had taken a GLP-1 reported weight loss.Of those who reported weight loss, time on treatment made a difference. Overall, 71% of respondents who had taken a GLP-1 for 6 months or fewer reported losing weight, compared with 80% of those who\u00e2\u0080\u0099d taken the medication for more than 7 months.That difference may reflect the fact that GLP-1s are often prescribed as a long-term treatment for obesity or diabetes. The body typically needs time to adjust to the medication before changes become noticeable.When it came to appetite control, people\u00e2\u0080\u0099s experiences closely matched their expectations. Many reported feeling less hungry or experiencing less \u00e2\u0080\u009cfood noise,\u00e2\u0080\u009d a common term for persistent or intrusive thoughts about food. Of those considering a GLP-1, 63% expected reduced food cravings, while 59% of those currently or formerly taking one reported it. This remained fairly consistent across treatment stages.In general, many people appeared to expect GLP-1 weight loss to unlock a much broader change in how they felt every day.Nearly half of the people considering treatment expected weight loss to bring more energy or improved mobility, but only 29% of people who had taken a GLP-1 reported these benefits. In contrast, 29% of people who had taken a GLP-1 reported fatigue as a side effect.Improved self-confidencewas also reported less often than people expected from a GLP-1.Fifty percent of people considering treatment anticipated improved confidence or body image, but only 37% of those who had taken a GLP-1 reported experiencing these benefits. The share was higher among people who had taken a GLP-1 for at least 7 months and had lost weight: 44% reported improved confidence or body image.A similar gap appeared for sleep quality. While 35% of people anticipated better sleep if they took a GLP-1, only 20% of those who took a GLP-1 reported this benefit.\u00c2\u00bbRead more:Understanding the Relationship Between Body Image and GLP-1sWhy this mattersThe Healthline Survey cannot tell us whether staying on a GLP-1 longer inevitably leads to greater weight loss or overall benefits. But the results do suggest that the first 3 months on a GLP-1 may not always provide a complete picture when assessing treatment success.Results during the first few weeks of GLP-1 treatment may look quite different from results closer to the 1-year mark, so treatment progress may need to be viewed within a longer timeline.The findings also suggest that losing weight with GLP-1s isn\u00e2\u0080\u0099t always accompanied by improvements in well-being.For this reason, understanding whether new symptoms are related to medication side effects or the consequences of rapid weight loss is essential for preventing and managing them effectively. It\u00e2\u0080\u0099s also important to recognize when a GLP-1 side effect may be serious or affect quality of life, as this can help identify opportunities to seek professional support, rather than discontinue GLP-1 treatment.For example, fatigue with GLP-1s is often linked to eating too little, inadequate nutrient intake, dehydration, sleep problems, or other underlying health factors. Similarly, joint pain or limited mobility may persist even after weight changes if other forms of support aren\u00e2\u0080\u0099t in place. Lifestyle factors, such as activity levels and activity type, may also influence these challenges.\u00e2\u0080\u009cFeeling better\u00e2\u0080\u009d may also be too broad to use as a GLP-1 treatment goal. More specific goals provide a clearer way to evaluate progress.Depending on why a GLP-1 medication is prescribed, goals that may be easier to measure include: changes in appetiteA1C levelsdaily blood sugar markersbody mass index (BMI)ability to enjoy or complete certain activitiesTracking dose changes, side effects, and lifestyle modifications in context can provide a more useful view of progress and treatment results.\u00c2\u00bbRead more:How Do GLP-1s Treat Type 2 Diabetes?Treatment length and post-treatment experience are top concernsAmong people currently taking a GLP-1 for weight loss, the Healthline Survey found that 67% expected to stop treatment within 2 years, compared with 35% of those taking the medication for diabetes or blood sugar management. That suggests people may plan GLP-1 treatment differently depending on why they started.Concerns about side effects, unknown long-term effects, and the cost of GLP-1s were among the reasons some people expected or planned for a shorter treatment timeline.Fifty-six percent of all people surveyed worried about regaining weight after stopping GLP-1s. That concern rose to 68% among people considering starting treatment.\u00e2\u0080\u009cI wish I knew how hard it would be to keep the weight off. Originally, I did not know about that until my first break a few years ago, which is why I got back on.\u00e2\u0080\u009c\u00e2\u0080\u0094 Respondent from the 2026 Healthline GLP-1 Survey, currently taking a GLP-1 (7-12 months)Why this mattersGLP-1 medications affect appetite and fullness. When treatment ends, those effects tend to fade. Hunger may return, and maintaining weight loss and blood sugar levels without any other aid can become difficult for many.Although the Healthline Survey did not measure how many people actually regained weight after stopping a GLP-1, reported concerns are consistent with findings from clinical research.In an extension of the STEP 1 trial, participants regained two-thirds of the weight they had lost within 1 year of stopping semaglutide (Wegovy, Ozempic).In the SURMOUNT-4 trial, people who stopped tirzepatide (Mounjaro, Zepbound) regained weight during the following year, while those who continued treatment lost additional weight.These results and the Healthline Survey findings suggest that a \u00e2\u0080\u009cstopping plan\u00e2\u0080\u009d may need to begin way before the last dose.Someone considering discontinuing a GLP-1 could benefit from reviewing the specifics of their medication and working with a healthcare professional to determine:what to monitor and track during treatmenthow to manage returning hunger or changes in blood sugarwhat lifestyle habits can sustain weight and blood sugar management long termwhether another treatment or support may be needed\u00c2\u00bbRead more:How to Track Weight Loss on a GLP-1 MedicationWhat the 2026 Healthline Survey adds to the GLP-1 conversationThe Healthline Survey\u00e2\u0080\u0099s most useful contribution may be to provide a reference point between two extremes: the broad averages found in clinical research and the highly individual stories shared by people taking GLP-1s.Here are the main conclusions:Real-world GLP-1 experiences can help ground expectationsPersonal stories reveal aspects of treatment that clinical trials may miss. At the same time, widely shared experiences often highlight extreme outcomes, like dramatic weight loss, severe nausea, extreme facial changes, or rapid weight regain. While realistic for some, these do not represent everyone\u00e2\u0080\u0099s experience with a GLP-1.Treatment decisions merit a multi-factor viewThe findings may also give healthcare professionals and people considering or taking a GLP-1 a way to distinguish among three different questions: What is possible with a GLP-1? Clinical trials and prescribing information help establish the benefits and risks observed under defined conditions. Individual experiences can vary, though. What appears common in the real world? The Healthline GLP-1 Survey captures self-reported experiences from people at different stages of treatment, offering a broader real-world view beyond clinical trials. While the sample is not representative of everyone taking or considering a GLP-1, it highlights diverse treatment journeys. What happens in individual cases? Personal factors such as goals, symptoms, cost, and quality of life can shape treatment choices, experiences, and medication adherence. That distinction can help you evaluate treatment more effectively. Instead of just asking if a GLP-1 is \u00e2\u0080\u009cworking,\u00e2\u0080\u009d you may want to track:goals you\u00e2\u0080\u0099ve metexpected benefits that have not appearedside effects affecting daily lifenext steps Read more: A Guide for Deciding If GLP-1s Are Right for YouGLP-1 treatment benefits from ongoing supportThe Healthline GLP-1 Survey suggests treatment planning should extend beyond the prescription stage.At first, you may need help with dose escalation, nutrition, hydration, physical activity, and symptom tracking. But ongoing support can make a difference in someone\u00e2\u0080\u0099s experience with GLP-1s, including managing side effects and staying on track.If you decide to stop taking a GLP-1, you may benefit from developing a long-term plan for managing changes in weight, appetite, and blood sugar levels. You may also need a plan to manage the return of symptoms that the medication was meant to help treat.Evidence and anecdotes help when put in contextHealthline\u00e2\u0080\u0099s GLP-1 Survey also offers a reason to approach striking online claims and narratives with curiosity rather than alarm.A single post shows one person\u00e2\u0080\u0099s experience, but it cannot establish how likely that outcome is for everyone else. Recognizing that distinction is an important first step toward putting individual experiences in context.Reliable health guidance and education can come from healthcare professionals, clinical research, prescribing information, and reputable health publishers. Personal experiences can add another perspective, but each source has different strengths and limitations.Distinguishing information that puts treatment expectations into context from anecdotes describing one person\u00e2\u0080\u0099s experience is important when considering treatment options. Realistic expectations do not remove every uncertainty, but they can provide a clearer framework for navigating treatment.Clearer expectations can help you track progress, manage side effects, and make decisions based on your needs rather than social conversations or online stories.\u00c2\u00bbRead more:How to Determine If Health Information Online Is TrustworthyThis article includes proprietary data from RVOH Consumer Surveys, which we conduct regularly to better understand real-world health challenges. This research provides context alongside external scientific studies, but it may not reflect all individual experiences. For more on how we gather this information, check the RVO Health Consumer Surveys Methodology.Disclaimer: While Healthline strives for factual, comprehensive, and current consumer drug information, this article is not a substitute for a licensed healthcare professional\u00e2\u0080\u0099s expertise. Always consult a doctor before taking or discontinuing any medication. Drug information is subject to change and may not cover all uses, directions, precautions, warnings, interactions, reactions, or adverse effects. The lack of a warning does not guarantee a drug\u00e2\u0080\u0099s safety, effectiveness, or appropriateness for all patients or specific uses. Learn more about our approach to content integrity.", "content_hash": "dd66a2712c42df018303410df987c9f867bfccbd99d0c1de642a95bb9693c859", "domain": "healthline.com", "fetched_at": "2026-10-09T05:18:34.714538+00:00", "language": ""}, {"id": "1403", "title": "Disposal of Landed Properties and Other Physical Assets, Defunct Primary Mortgage Banks", "url": "https://ndic.gov.ng/article?slug=notice-to-general-public-for-disposal-of-landed-properties-and-other-physical-assets-of-defunct-primary-mortgage-banks", "final_url": "https://ndic.gov.ng/article?slug=notice-to-general-public-for-disposal-of-landed-properties-and-other-physical-assets-of-defunct-primary-mortgage-banks", "text": "Disposal of Landed Properties and Other Physical Assets, Defunct Primary Mortgage Banks N NDIC June 19, 2026 3 min read The Nigeria Deposit Insurance Corporation (NDIC), in the exercise of its statutory powers as liquidator of failed banks under section 62(1)(d) of NDIC Act, 2023 as amended, wishes to draw the attention of the general public for the upcoming disposal of landed properties belonging to Aso Savings & Loans and Union Homes Savings & Loans, at three (3) affected locations across the country. The scheduled dates for the bid opening exercise is Thursday, 2nd July, 2026.The scope of disposal will be for the bidding of landed properties belonging to Aso Savings & Loans and Union Homes Savings & Loans (In-Liquidation) across the country. Buyers who wish to participate shall follow laid down guidelines which is aimed at transparency, fair competition, accountability, equity and timeliness in line with the tenets of corporate governance principles to enable recovery of commensurate financial returns from the auction exercise for the payment of liquidation dividends to uninsured depositors and creditors accordingly.The exercise is the disposal of three (3) Landed Properties belonging to the defunct Aso Savings & Loans and Union Homes Savings & Loans across the country. The disposal exercise would be transparent to all interested bidders.Landed properties in the affected location as listed are to be disposed therein. The property will be sold \u201cas is \u201cand \u201cnet \u201cof all taxes.The bidding exercise shall be open and competitive to all bidders and shall take place at the Mamman Kontagora House, 23A Marina, Lagos.Where the property is being occupied, the sitting tenant would be given preference of first refusal before other bidders can be considered.All interested bidders should submit their sealed bids together with 10% bid security in Bank Draft at the NDIC Lagos Office, Asset Management Department, (9thFloor), Mamman Kontagora House, 23A, Marina, Lagos. Failure to attach the 10% Bank Draft for bid security will render such submitted bids Null and Void. Please note that any winning bidders who failed to pay the balance of 90% within two (2) weeks shall forfeit the deposited 10% bid security sum.All financial bids should be dropped in the bid box provided for this purpose on the 9thfloor, Mamman Kontagora House, Marina Lagos on or before Thursday, 2nd July, 20262 by 10 am prompt while the bid opening shall be by 11 am prompt.All interested bidders and general public are invited to the bid opening session at the designated location.Find below the detailed locations of the Landed Properties.S/NBANK NAMESTATEDESCRIPRTIONLOCATION1.ASO SAVINGS & LOANSAbujaFully Detached 1-Story Building and a boys Quarters including ChattelsNo 9, Ogbomosho Street, Area 8, Garki, Abuja.2.ASO SAVINGS & LOANSAbujaA Fully Detached BungalowPlot No. 341B Kuje Action Layout, Kuje Area Council, Abuja.3.UNION HOMES SAVINGS & LOANSOyoFully Detached 1-Story Building and a boys Quarters including Chattels32 New Court Road, Old Gbagi Market, Ibadan North-West LGA, Dugbe, Ibadan For further enquiries, please contact:Director, Asset Management Department,Nigeria Deposit Insurance Corporation, Lagos Office,Mamman Kotangora House (9th Floor),23A, Marina,Lagos.Tel No: 08035925533Lagos Office Signed: Management All Articles Share More Articles July 22, 2026 Hon. Minister of Finance's Keynote Speech at IADI-ARC Workshop 2026 July 21, 2026 MD/CE NDIC\u2019S Welcome Remark at IADI-ARC Workshop & Annual Meeting 2026 July 17, 2026 NDIC Hosts IADI Africa Regional Committee Meeting 2026 July 1, 2026 Update on the Revocation of the Operating Licenses of 46 Micro-Finance Banks by the Central Bank of Nigeria June 15, 2026 Sale of Chattels of Closed Primary Mortgage Banks by Public Auction and Public Competitive Bid. Browse all articles", "content_hash": "c312a6997eb64f94394a31c5b3885ad988af5f1262796362c8a6926d3c0a2f5e", "domain": "ndic.gov.ng", "fetched_at": "2026-10-09T05:18:35.759577+00:00", "language": ""}]}